# Agent and contributor workflow

Status: operational procedure. How work is done in this repository: the agent playbooks, the
rules they share, and [concurrent work](#concurrent-work-one-worktree-per-writer) (one worktree per
writer, the server gate runs, publishing). It absorbed `CONCURRENT_WORK.md` on 2026-10-29. The
tool manuals it points to stay separate: [LANDING.md](docs/LANDING.md) (landing trains and the bot),
[REVIEW.md](docs/REVIEW.md) (`scripts/review/review.sh`), [WORK_QUEUE.md](legacy/docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/WORK_QUEUE.md) (the agent work
queue), [STATUS_PAGE.md](legacy/docs/project/STATUS_PAGE.md) and [PROMOTION_PIPELINE.md](legacy/docs/project/PROMOTION_PIPELINE.md) (the
validation stages, CI tiers and result vocabulary).

**Since the legacy/ move (R-0558).** The old compiler and its gates are frozen in `legacy/` and never
run. Where this document names a path that is not at the root (`scripts/tests/`, `scripts/gen/`,
`docs/bugs/`, `Concrete/` and the like), it now lives under `legacy/` and describes the old
compiler. The checks a push, CI and the train run are `scripts/checks/run.sh`. A defect in the
spec, the proofs, the tooling or CI is a row in [ISSUES.tsv](ISSUES.tsv); `/file-bug` and `/fix-bug`
are archived with the old bug ledger (`legacy/.claude/commands/`).

## Playbooks

Project playbooks live in `.claude/commands/`. Every agent session, on the laptop or the server,
runs the same procedure by name instead of receiving a long prompt:

| command | what it does |
|---|---|
| `/fix-bug <N or description>` | reproduce, classify, repair or contain one bug on its own branch |
| `/roadmap-item <R-NNNN>` | carry one roadmap task to a landable candidate |
| `/file-bug <title>` | file a new bug with `scripts/gen/new_bug.sh` |
| `/creview` | Codex review of the branch through `scripts/review/review.sh` |
| `/rebase` | the safe rebase onto `origin/main` |
| `/land` | hand a validated branch to the landing train (`scripts/land/train.sh`) |
| `/cstatus` | where this worktree, its branch, its CI and this machine stand |

`/creview` and `/cstatus` carry a `c` prefix so they do not shadow Claude Code's built-in `/review`
and `/status`; a new playbook takes a name no built-in uses.

This file owns the rules the commands share. A command links here rather than restating them, so a
rule changes in one place.

## Rules

**Design questions** are decided by [PHILOSOPHY.md](docs/PHILOSOPHY.md). Any important design decision is
put to the owner by Claude or Codex, with a recommendation reasoned from docs/PHILOSOPHY.md.

**Owner decisions: never decide one.** The language decisions OD1 – OD157
([decisions.md](docs/decisions.md#owner-decisions)) are decided only by the project owner. No
agent, orchestrator or reviewer chooses, encodes, implements or provisionally ships any side of any
of them, inside a bug fix or anywhere else. Agents present options and a recommendation to the owner
and wait for an explicit answer. Behaviour already on main that took a side stays as it is, recorded
as a conflict, until the owner answers; nobody extends or reverts it meanwhile.
- The Codex review of every fix (`scripts/review/review.sh` asks it) also answers: "does this fix
  pick a side on any OD in docs/decisions.md?" If yes, or if the fix cannot be made without picking
  one, stop and report to the owner; do not land it. Recording an answer the owner explicitly gave
  is not picking a side; what the review checks then is in
  [REVIEW.md](docs/REVIEW.md#owner-decisions-choosing-versus-recording).

**Bugs.**
- Every bug gets a structural cause and a structural owner: the design fact that let it exist, and
  the roadmap task or pipeline stage that removes the whole class
  ([STRUCTURAL_CLASSES.md](legacy/docs/bugs/STRUCTURAL_CLASSES.md)). A patch without them is not a fix.
- To file a bug, run `scripts/gen/new_bug.sh`, which writes its record and runs the ledger
  generator. To log a change, add a `changelog.d/` fragment
  ([changelog.d/README.md](legacy/changelog.d/README.md)). Never hand-edit a generated view (the bug
  lists, the class table's bugs column, the generated bug dashboard, `CHANGELOG.md`,
  `Concrete/BuildIdentity.lean`); see [scripts/tests/README.md](legacy/scripts/tests/README.md),
  "Shared ledgers".
- Each bug's compiler area is derived from its fix commits ([AREAS.md](legacy/docs/bugs/AREAS.md);
  `python3 scripts/gen/bug_ledger.py report` for the stage, class, month, silent, how-found and
  time-to-fix tables, `--json` or `--sqlite PATH` for queries). Name the bug in the subject of each
  commit that fixes it (`bug NNN: ...`), and after committing a fix rerun
  `scripts/gen/refresh_generated.sh` and commit the regenerated AREAS.md. Add an `area:` line to a
  record only when the record shows the derived area is wrong
  ([README.md](legacy/docs/bugs/README.md#bug-areas)); never fill areas in by hand otherwise.
- For `compiler: new`, a silent defect (wrong code, unsound claim) is stopped at once by repair
  or fail-closed containment; a loud one is filed, pinned and owned. Legacy-compiler defects
  are recorded against `legacy-compiler-final`, never repaired, under
  [ROADMAP's defect disposition and frozen-compiler rule](ROADMAP.md#defect-disposition-rule).

**Branches.**
- One bug or one rule per branch, in its own worktree (`scripts/worktree-new.sh`,
  [concurrent work](#concurrent-work-one-worktree-per-writer)). This applies to all agent work now, not only once
  R-0545 closes. A branch that changes a language rule lands first and stays small; work that
  depends on it waits or rebases onto it.
- Rebase onto `origin/main` after every landing (`/rebase`), not only before your own landing, so a
  conflict or a changed rule shows up while the branch is small.
- Commit each validated slice. Push the branch to `wip/<branch>` at least every 5 minutes of work
  (`git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/wip/<branch>`); the hook's wip tier only scans for conflict
  markers.
- Never push to `main`, `ci/*` or the remote `fork`. Landings go to both `origin` and
  `lambdaclass`, only through the landing script (`scripts/land/train.sh`) or the landing bot.
  `train.sh check` runs the checks before the push, and the push runs the root hook's full set again;
  the train's push to `ci/<name>` and both pushes of `land` are read back with `ls-remote` and
  retried when they did not move the ref (GitHub drops the connection during long hooks); `land` checks that both remotes are at
  the head ([LANDING.md](docs/LANDING.md#land)).
- After any push of your own whose hook ran gates, check that the remote ref moved
  (`git ls-remote origin refs/heads/<branch>`) and push again if it did not.

**Validation.**
- Locally, run only the impact-selected gates (pre-push runs the fast surface gates and the gates
  selected by what the push touches). For the full set, dispatch CI on the branch
  (`gh workflow run CI --ref <branch>`) or use the server (`concrete-remote wip/<branch> 'lake build
  && bash scripts/checks/run.sh'`); never a full local run on the laptop.
- Heavy legs default to the CI server (concrete-ci-2). Set `git config land.gatesHost concrete-ci-2`
  in the clone you land from, and `train.sh check` then runs its impact gates there, bound to the
  train head's SHA. The `ci` push still runs the root hook's full set; it reuses no remote result. An unreachable host is INCOMPLETE and blocks;
  run locally only with `check --local-gates` (docs/LANDING.md, "Gate host").
- At most 2 heavy runs (full suites, mutation campaigns, `lake build` of the whole tree) per machine.
  Queue behind a running one; continue light work meanwhile. Honour a user's explicit CPU limits.
- Run gates inside `nix develop`. A gate that passes by hand but fails under the hook: suspect an
  inherited `GIT_*` variable first.
- Record each result against the exact commit SHA it ran on.
- Read the obligation, not the prose: a header says what was true when it was written. Make gates
  assert properties, not counts, so an improvement does not read as a regression.

**Before asking for review.** Each of these caused an integration failure in the week to
2026-10-25; run them on the slice, in `nix develop`, before `/creview`:
- `scripts/tests/test_golden.sh` after any change to compiler output or a report.
- `scripts/tests/test_mutation.sh --check-patterns` after editing any file a mutation targets: it
  finds an anchor the edit broke in seconds, before a mutation campaign fails on it.
- `scripts/tests/check_type_identity.sh` and the other gates that elaborate Lean probes against the
  compiler (`grep -l 'lake env lean' scripts/tests/check_*.sh`) after changing a signature or a
  structure they construct.
- A fixture's `run_ok` / `run_err` leg in `run_tests.sh` spells its file name literally: the
  bug-corpus audit looks for each fixture's basename in `run_tests.sh`, so a name assembled from
  parts is reported as uncovered.
- Bound a command with `bounded_run` (`scripts/checks/lib/bounded.sh`), never GNU `timeout`: the
  macOS job has no `timeout`, and `bounded_run` reports a second timeout as INCOMPLETE, not a pass.
- A ledger `coverage:` line names only the gates that cover the bug: the filing check accepts a
  `.sh` name there as the coverage claim without running or opening it.
- File in the migrated ledger format with `scripts/gen/new_bug.sh`, which writes the `bug-ledger`
  block, a `changelog.d/` fragment and the `discovery.tsv` row (`--batch` or `--new-batch`, with
  `--silent`) and regenerates the views; after editing a record, regenerate with
  `scripts/gen/bug_ledger.py --write` and `scripts/gen/bug_dashboard.py --write`. If the script fails
  after reserving a number, rerun the `--number NNN` command it prints rather than filing by hand.
- Have Codex confirm each bug is real (a reproducer and a verdict line in the record) before
  filing it.

**Review.** Codex reviews everything before it is published or brought to the owner, and a second
independent reviewer reviews too when one is available; its absence is recorded, never assumed
([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-review)). Every slice gets a Codex review before it lands, through `scripts/review/review.sh`:
at most 5 rounds, the first over the whole diff and each later round over the delta since the
previous one. Answer each finding with a fix or a stated reason. Run Codex by hand only with
`GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0` (and `</dev/null`): its sandboxed `git status` otherwise takes the
worktree's `index.lock` and can leave it behind (bug 209).

**Git safety.**
- Never pipe `git rebase` (or `git merge`, `git cherry-pick`) into anything: a pipe hides the stop.
  After every git operation, check that no merge, rebase, cherry-pick or revert is in progress
  (`git status`; `MERGE_HEAD`, `rebase-merge`, `rebase-apply` under `git rev-parse --git-dir`).
- No bare `git stash`; no interactive git (`-i`).
- Check a process's working directory (`lsof -a -p <pid> -d cwd`) before killing it; many processes
  on this machine belong to other repositories and other sessions.
- Remove a stale `index.lock` only when no process owns it (`lsof <path>/index.lock` is empty);
  otherwise wait and retry.
- Preserve other sessions' edits and untracked files. Never reset or clean a shared checkout.

**Language invariants.** Link to the normative documents instead of restating them:
[OWNERSHIP_MODEL.md](legacy/docs/archive/design-records/OWNERSHIP_MODEL.md) (linear, not affine: non-Copy values are used
exactly once), [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) (strictly second-class
references, §15), [the spec's overview](legacy/docs/spec/overview.md),
[STATEMENT_EXPRESSION_MODEL.md](legacy/docs/archive/design-records/STATEMENT_EXPRESSION_MODEL.md) (evaluation order, value
vs statement position), [KNOWN_HOLES.md](legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) (the only authority on
hole status) and [VERIFIED_PIPELINE.md](legacy/docs/compiler/VERIFIED_PIPELINE.md) (the structural owners).

## Concurrent work: one worktree per writer

**The default: every concurrent line of work gets its own git worktree.**

```sh
scripts/worktree-new.sh <name> [base-committish]
```

This is not an optimization. One working tree cannot serve two writers, because
the file system is the shared mutable state and neither writer can see the
other's intent.

### What went wrong when this was not the default

On 2026-07-31 two agents were found working in the same worktree
(`.claude/worktrees/r0442-proofcore-callee`). Every symptom was initially
misread as something else:

| symptom | what it was blamed on | what it actually was |
| --- | --- | --- |
| an edit to `ProofCore.lean` vanished, and the file dropped out of `git status` | the mutation harness | the mutation harness restoring a backup **over a concurrent edit**, then verifying against the pre-edit hash and reporting success |
| `origin/main` moved three times mid-session | stale fetches | the other agent pushing |
| two `push-both.sh` runs waiting on CI for the same SHA | a duplicated invocation | two agents each publishing |

The first row is the expensive one: work was destroyed *and* the tool reported
that nothing was wrong. A postcondition that compares against a stale baseline
confirms the corruption it was meant to catch.

### What a worktree does and does not isolate

| | |
| --- | --- |
| **Isolated** | working tree, index, `HEAD`, current branch |
| **Shared** | object store, remotes, **the stash stack**, branch namespace |

So worktrees fix file-level races, not coordination:

- Pushes must still be sequenced — hence the lock in `push-both.sh`.
- `git stash` is still unsafe across worktrees; bare `git stash` / `git stash pop`
  is banned repo-wide. Use a temporary WIP commit, or
  `git stash push -u -m "<unique-tag>"` and recover by SHA with `apply`, never
  `pop`.
- A branch name is a shared resource. `worktree-new.sh` refuses to reuse one
  rather than let `git` fail halfway, because the existing branch may belong to
  another writer.

### Enforcement is at the point of danger, not by head-count

Two heuristics for "is another agent here?" were measured and both cried wolf:

- matching process names (`pgrep -lf "codex|claude"`) reported **115** — every
  shell wrapper's command line contains the `.claude` snapshot path;
- counting processes whose CWD is the tree reported **7** in a worktree freshly
  created by a single agent — its own shells and background waiters all sit there.

Neither distinguishes "two writers" from "one agent working normally", and a
warning that fires on non-events trains the reader to ignore the one that
matters. So concurrency is caught where the signal is a fact:

- **`scripts/tests/test_mutation.sh`** records a hash of the content it itself
  wrote. At restore time, content that is neither its mutation nor the original
  means a third party wrote the file: it **refuses to restore**, preserves their
  version under `CONCURRENT-EDIT/`, keeps its own backup for reconciliation, and
  fails the run. It cannot silently overwrite an edit it did not make.
- **`scripts/push-both.sh`** takes an exclusive lock in the **common** git dir
  (`git rev-parse --git-common-dir`), not the worktree — remotes are shared, so a
  per-worktree lock would miss two worktrees publishing at once.

### Working while something long is running

Long-running work does not always require an idle tree. Distinguish the phases:

| phase | tree must be stable? |
| --- | --- |
| pre-push hook running gates | **yes** — gates read the working tree |
| `run_ci_gates_local.sh` | **yes** — same reason |
| `test_mutation.sh` | **yes** — it rewrites source in place |
| `push-both.sh` waiting for remote CI | **no** — it only polls `gh` |

The CI wait touches no files, so work can continue during it — in another
worktree. Treating the whole publish as tree-freezing is over-caution; treating
the hook phase as safe is the error that costs a run.

### Landing, review and load (development throughput)

On 2026-10-22 a finished branch took 6 – 10 h to reach main for about 1 h of work.
The [development throughput](ROADMAP.md#development-throughput) track in the
roadmap owns the repair. These rules apply to every writer now:

- **One bug or one rule per branch** (R-0545). A branch that changes a language or
  checker rule lands first and small, with the migrations of main's fixtures and
  nothing else. A branch that needs two rules is two branches.
- **Review per slice, delta after the first pass** (R-0544). After the first full
  review, each round reviews only the diff since the previous round. Past 5 rounds,
  split the slice. Review ownership and memory-safety work with neutral prompts and
  piecewise diffs.
- **At most 2 heavy local runs per machine** (R-0543). Full gate sets, closure-mode
  runs and mutation campaigns count as heavy. Agents run the fast and
  impact-selected gates; full sets go to the servers (R-0541) or to CI. The
  machine-wide semaphore `scripts/checks/lib/heavy_slots.sh` holds the full runners,
  `run_tests.sh --full` and the mutation drivers to two slots; for any other heavy
  command, check the load and queue behind a running heavy run instead of starting a third.
- **Ready branches land together** (R-0539). Ready branches are integrated into one
  train candidate with one CI run, rather than each rebasing onto the last landing,
  through `scripts/land/train.sh` ([LANDING.md](docs/LANDING.md)). A train built by hand
  follows the same rules: one integrated candidate, results recorded against its
  exact SHA, and bisection by member only on red.

### Running the full gate set on the server

The full set runs on a server (or in CI), never on the laptop:

```
~/bin/concrete-remote <pushed-ref> \
  'lake build && bash scripts/checks/run.sh'
```

`concrete-remote` checks the ref out as a `git clone --shared` (not a worktree:
two mutation-driver gates refuse worktrees) and sets the runner's default width
to the measured best for that host (`CONCRETE_GATE_JOBS_DEFAULT`; override with
`CONCRETE_REMOTE_JOBS=N`, or pass `JOBS=N` in the command). The measurements and
the critical path were taken on concrete-ci-1, since deleted (2026-10-23), and are
in [GATE_PROFILE.md](legacy/docs/project/GATE_PROFILE.md#parallel-scaling-on-concrete-ci-1-r-0546-step-4).

**A gate must be safe to run beside every other gate.** In a pass the gates run
JOBS-wide in one checkout, and several passes run on one host. So a gate:

- writes scratch only under `mktemp -d` (or a `.$$` name), never a fixed
  `/tmp/<name>` — `check_gate_hygiene.sh` refuses fixed `/tmp` paths;
- never edits a tracked file in place, not even to restore it a moment later.
  A probe that perturbs a source runs in a private copy of the checkout
  (`run_fast_surface_gates.sh --mutate`, `check_attestation_manifest.sh`). An
  in-place edit of a `.lean` file makes every concurrent `require_fresh_binary`
  rebuild the shared compiler from it;
- runs sub-steps concurrently only under one lock it already holds (fresh.sh
  refuses a second concurrent acquisition) and records each sub-step's exit
  status, so a lost result is a failure, not a pass.

A pool failure that passes on the runner's sequential re-check is listed under
"Recovered under sequential re-run" and in `.ci-gate-timings.tsv` (stage
`pool`, verdict `FAIL`, then stage `recheck`). Treat it as a race in that gate
to fix, not as noise.

### Killing a publish does not un-publish it

`push-both.sh` runs `git push` as a CHILD process. Killing the script does not
kill that child, and a `git push` already in flight will complete.

Measured 2026-07-31: a publish was killed during its pre-push hook, `git ls-remote`
showed the primary unchanged, and that was read as "nothing was published". The
`git push` child was still running; it finished moments later and advanced the
primary. The next publish was then rejected as non-fast-forward — harmless here,
but the reasoning behind the kill was a race read as a guarantee.

So: a ref check taken *at* the moment of the kill proves nothing about a push
that has already started. If a publish must be abandoned, re-check the remote
AFTER the process tree is gone (`pgrep -f "git push"` clear), and treat the
remote's actual state as the answer rather than the state you saw before killing.

### Orchestrating a publish: never infer state from process names

`pgrep -f "push-both"` matches the WAITER'S OWN command line, which contains that
string. Every `until ! pgrep -f "push-both"; do sleep 30; done` loop therefore
waits on itself forever, and a status check written the same way reports "publish
running" when nothing is. Measured 2026-08-01: six background tasks were stuck on
this at once, and the reported state was wrong for as long as they ran. It is the
same failure as counting `pgrep -lf "codex|claude"` and getting 115 — a
process-name match catches the matcher.

Do this instead:

* capture the launched PID and `wait "$pid"`;
* verify completion by checking the EXACT REMOTE SHA (`git ls-remote`), which is
  the fact you actually care about;
* query CI for that SHA (`gh run list --commit <full-sha>` — the full SHA; the
  short form returns nothing);
* never infer state from a process-name match.

For a one-off inspection, `ps -eo command | grep -c "[p]ush-both.sh"` excludes
itself via the bracket, but a ref check is still the better answer.

### Reviewing a branch

Every agent reviews the same way: `scripts/review/review.sh`, one round per
invocation, with the verdict recorded as a git note on the reviewed commit. The
note is shared through `origin`, so rounds run in another worktree or by another
agent count toward the same cap, and the next round reviews only the delta. See
[REVIEW.md](docs/REVIEW.md).
# Reading the Concrete specification

**Status: PROPOSED.** This is the specification of the new compiler, written in Lean (decision
rows Q9.15, P3: Lean is the specification). It is not normative until the owner freezes Core at
stage A2c (row Q9.19). Before that, the owner approved the Core design provisionally (A2a,
2026-11-05), and A2b formalized it here and checked the four design theorems (row Q9.14).

## How to read it

[`Spec.lean`](Spec.lean) is the specification library (row Q9.15; split into modules by owner
decision 2026-11-06). It imports the modules under [`Spec/`](Spec/) in reading order, and each
module's header says what it covers. Every rule is a Lean definition with a plain-English doc
comment, followed by example programs: the surface program in a doc comment, its Core form, and
what the rules say about it: accepted (`check p = true`) with its outcome (`run p 10 = .exit 42`),
or refused (`check p = false`). You can read it without reading any proof.

[`Proofs/`](Proofs/) says why the rules hang together. Read it only to check a claim.

**The owner view.** `scripts/spec/lean_site.sh [OUT]` (after `lake build ConcreteSpec`) generates
a static HTML view into `_build/spec-site/`: every module rendered from its source (doc comments as
prose, rules and examples as code with linked names), each declaration with what it uses and which
proofs are about it, and a page of all theorems with their statements. It is generated from the
sources and the elaborated environment only, and fails on a broken link.

The decisions are cited by row id (Q1.1 … Q9.19, P1 … P23, in
[docs/decisions.md](../docs/decisions.md)) and by rule id (ODnn, TYPE-n, OWN-n, …, in the
archived prose specification [legacy/docs/spec](../legacy/docs/spec)).

## Rules of the file

- Only Lean core is imported, no Mathlib (Q9.16). No `sorry`, no `native_decide` (Q9.17), and
  `autoImplicit` is off, so a misspelled name is an error, never a silently bound variable.
- Axioms: only Lean's standard `propext`, `Quot.sound` and `Classical.choice`. The evaluator
  `run`, the checker `check` and the other executable definitions use `propext` alone, so they
  stay executable and the examples are decided by running them; `Classical.choice` appears only
  in proofs (`rep_roundtrip` through the core lemma `Nat.mod_mul`, and the range proofs).
- These rules are checked in CI by [scripts/proofs](../scripts/proofs): the per-theorem axiom
  inventory against its golden list, the transitive ban on `sorry` and `native_decide`, a fresh
  kernel replay with `leanchecker`, and an independent re-check of the exported theorems by
  con-leche and nanoda.
- Every rule a diagnostic can name is registered in [`Spec/Rules.lean`](Spec/Rules.lean) by its
  Lean name and revision. The rule gate ([scripts/spec/rules.sh](../scripts/spec/rules.sh), run by
  CI's Spec build) requires each registered rule to cite its decision row and to have an example
  it accepts and one it refuses, and every rule citation to name a registered rule.
- The judgments (inductive relations) are the normative form; the checker is proved to agree with
  them (`check_iff`).
- Build: `lake build ConcreteSpec`. The external checker re-checks every theorem.

## What remains open

The parts are separate Core fragments, each defined only as far as its theorems need (R4 F8);
unifying them into one Core is A2i work (R-0571).

Open obligations, by name:

- **O1 Elaboration** from surface syntax to Core: how literals get a type, how names become
  identities, how nested expressions and grouping become A-normal form, source positions, and that
  acceptance of a source program does not depend on how the elaborator names temporaries.
- **O2 The lexer, model-translation and native-image obligations.**
- **O3 Instrumentation erasure** (Q9.10): the ownership instrumentation (`Slot.moved`, the
  `used`, `live`, `known` and `kfields` fields of `Entry`) is stated as intent, with no erasure
  definition or theorem yet. (Brand erasure, part 5, is done.)
- **O4 Trap diagnostics** (Q7.10, OD101): the bytes of the trap line, including the trap's source
  location (needs O1's positions), and of the startup refusal.
- **O5 Target observations** (the launcher contract): every result here is about the model's
  outcomes; a claim about an executable needs a transport to the approved launcher contract.
- **O6 Launch validation** (part 6): `ProgOk` assumes a valid launch: instance limits, the target
  offering each parameter type, and the launcher's grant minting are not modelled; nor are
  authority rule 11, foreign calls, trait dispatch, generic methods and generated cleanup.
- **O7 Capabilities in the other parts**: the cleanup calls of part 3 and the allocator
  operations of part 5 need capabilities; part 6 checks capabilities in its own fragment only
  (unification, A2i).
- **O8 Part 3** abstracts each deferred call to its identity, dependencies, loans and whether it
  traps; it does not model the statements between registrations or evaluating a `return` value
  before cleanup.
- **O9 Part 4**: the run-time fragment (`vrun`) and the access rules (`Ok`) are separate
  fragments, not yet joined in one soundness theorem (A2i); the run covers array sources (text
  views share the index arithmetic, and their validity is `text_view_utf8`); views of views,
  writes through text views, and text whose length is known while its bytes are not (treated as
  unknown) are not modelled; the frame theorem covers bodies that access only their view. That
  compiled code performs the same range check is a compiler-slice obligation (O5).
- **O10 Part 5** abstracts the condition of `while`: loop test number `k` takes the outcome
  `σ k`, and the results hold for every `σ`.
- **O11 Foundation contracts** (owner decision 2026-11-08, C1–C10): written so far are C3
  (`Target`), C1 (`Exec`: the machine, its step, cleanup order, traps; parametric in the operand
  language) and C2 (`Scalar`, `Const`). C4–C10 remain. C1 leaves to later slices the source-level
  evaluation orders (elaboration, O1), counted loops (slice 4), and ownership of moved values
  (C5), the split of a request from its response (C6) and the oracle's query record (C8); its
  shared state is abstract until C4 and C5 instantiate it. C2 leaves floats (slice 26), the
  representation of scalars in bytes (C4, which ports `Rep` onto `Core.IntTy` so one scalar model
  remains), and two owner questions: whether a failing operation may appear in a constant
  initializer, and whether the source may name a constant written later in the file.
import ConcreteSpec.Spec.Scalar

/-!
# Execution, calls and cleanup

PROPOSED: not normative until the owner freezes Core (ROADMAP A2c). Part of `ConcreteSpec.Spec`;
see `ConcreteSpec/README.md`. Foundation contract C1 (the foundation proposal approved by the
owner on 2026-11-08, "ok all recommended", question 1: the interfaces and their laws are written,
proved and tested before the freeze).

This module is the shape of every Core execution: a machine configuration, one labeled step, and
the laws that sequencing, scopes, exits, calls and cleanup obey. It is *parametric* in the operand
language (`Operands`): what a value is, how an operand is evaluated, which types exist, what the
shared state is (memory, C4; the loan and ownership instrumentation, C5) and which primitive
operations read and change it or talk to the environment. The scalar operations (C2), memory (C4)
and the environment (C6) are instances; they add operations, never change what the statements below
mean (the freeze boundary: an extension preserves the old fragment's executions).

**The configuration.** A `Config` holds the current control (a statement to run, a statement just
completed, an exit in progress, or a trap), the locals, the shared state, and the stack of
continuation frames that says where execution resumes. Call frames are frames of the same stack. A
call saves its caller's locals and restores them on return; the shared state is *not* saved, so
what a callee (or a cleanup call) does to memory is seen after it returns.

**Places and moves.** Writing a place other than a local (a field, an element, memory behind a
pointer) is a primitive operation of the instance: its operands, the place's index operands and the
value, are evaluated before it runs, and in A-normal form elaboration has already evaluated the
right-hand side before any destination subexpression. Core's operands are explicit `copy x` and
`move x` (Q9.1): which a type allows, and that a moved value is used exactly once, is the ownership
instrumentation (C5), which is part of the instance's state; a move has no other run-time effect.

**Evaluation order** (C1, STMT-4/5). Core is in A-normal form (Q9.1): an operand has no run-time
effects, so after erasure (C9) the only order that can be observed among operands is which failure
is reported first; before erasure, the instrumentation also sees the left-to-right order (a later
operand sees what an earlier one moved). Arguments
are evaluated left to right, and the first that traps decides the trap (`evalArgs`); every argument
is evaluated before the callee is entered. An assignment evaluates its right-hand side before
storing it. Source-level orders (STMT-4: receiver before arguments, left operand before right, a
base before its index, an assignment's right-hand side before its destination's subexpressions,
fields and elements in written order, `&&` and `||` evaluating their right operand only when needed) are
elaboration's obligation: it lowers them to this sequential Core, and the checker checks both
operands of a short-circuit operator (Q6.5). That belongs to the elaboration slices (README O1).

**Calls.** A call names its callee by typed identity (`FnId`, never a string), evaluates its
arguments, and enters the callee with its parameters bound in order. There is no receiver: what a
source method calls its receiver is the callee's ordinary, typed first parameter (owner decision
2026-11-08, item 7). Destruction is one consuming protocol (`DestroyProtocol`): for a type with
`Destroy`, exactly one function, taking that type's value as its only parameter and returning
`Unit` (OD60, Q2.7).

**Exits and cleanup** (Q9.4, Q9.8). `break L` and `continue L` name their loop (Q9.3); `return`
evaluates its value *before* leaving any scope. On every structured exit (falling off the end of a
block, `break`, `continue`, `return`) the deferred calls of each scope the exit leaves run, the
innermost scope first and, within a scope, the newest registration first; their operands are read
when they run (late reads, Q9.8). A deferred call is an ordinary call: if it traps, the run stops at
once and no remaining cleanup runs. An explicit `abort()` is terminal: it runs no deferred call and
does not unwind. Ordinary locals get no implicit destructor: cleanup happens only through what a
scope registered (Q2.1 keeps release, destruction and resource cleanup apart).

**The loop** (Q9.3) is `while L { C } test t do { B }`: the condition region `C` runs, then the test
`t`; if it holds, the body runs and the loop starts again from `C`; `continue L` also starts again
from `C`. A counted loop is lowered to this form by elaboration, with its bound snapshot taken
before the loop and its counter advanced in `C` (slice 4). Exits inside a condition region are
restricted by the checker (Q9.3); the machine passes such an exit through the loop's test frame
without matching it. The temporaries the condition region binds (Q9.3's `t1 = next(...)`,
`t2 = t1 != 0`, test `t2`) are visible to the test and are dropped right after it, so every
iteration, the body and the code after the loop see only the locals the loop started with.

The laws are in `Proofs/Exec.lean`: sequencing, scope restoration (`lookup_restrict`), that an exit
carries its value unchanged through cleanup, that the deferred calls run in the stated order
(`exit_scope_runs_defers`, `unwind_runs_plan`), that a trap is terminal and that `abort()` runs no
cleanup.

`run` is the fuel-indexed driver of this step relation, the skeleton of the reference evaluator
(Q9.9); its adequacy (C8) is stated against `Step`.
-/

namespace ConcreteSpec.Core.Exec
open ConcreteSpec.Core

/-- How a primitive operation was answered: the event the environment saw, if any, the result, and
the new shared state. -/
structure Answer (Val Event Store : Type) where
  event : Option Event
  result : Except TrapKind Val
  store : Store

/-- A change in the life of locals, which an instrumentation may track (C5): a local is initialized
(by `bind`, by `assign`, or as the result of a call or a primitive operation), an activation starts
with its parameters initialized, the current activation ends, or locals go out of scope. The
machine reports each change to the instance (`Operands.life`), so ownership state can be kept per
activation and a reinitialized local can be told apart from a moved one. -/
inductive Life where
  | bind (x : Var)
  | enter (params : List Var)
  | leave
  | drop (xs : List Var)

/-- What an operand language supplies to the machine. An instance is a lawful extension: the
statements keep their meaning whatever the instance.

- `Val`, `Expr`: values and operands. `eval` evaluates an operand against the locals and the
  shared state: a value and the state after it, a trap, or unsafe stuckness (`Res`). An operand
  has no run-time effect (Core is in A-normal form, Q9.1); the state it returns may differ only in
  instrumentation, such as the ownership state C5 adds, where `move x` marks `x` moved. Erasure
  (C9) removes the instrumentation and must show the erased runs agree.
- `Store`: the shared state, threaded through operands, calls and cleanup.
- `life`: how a change in the life of locals (`Life`) changes the state. Like the state an
  operand returns, it may change only instrumentation; an instance without instrumentation
  ignores it.
- `test`: the truth value of a condition (`none`: not a condition; unsafe stuckness).
- `Ty`, `unitTy`, `unitVal`, `isCopy`: the types, `Unit` and its value, and which types are `Copy`
  (a declared and checked instance; the checker owns that check, C9).
- `Prim`, `allows`: the primitive operations and their contracts. `allows p args st a` says that a
  request `p args` in state `st` may be answered by `a`: an event the environment sees (or none,
  for an internal operation such as a memory access), a result (a value or a trap) and the new
  state. An answer outside the contract is never taken (C6): `Step` has no transition for it, so
  an operation with no allowed answer has none. The driver `runFrom` reports an oracle that gives
  no answer (`noAnswer`) apart from one whose answer is refused (`badAnswer`). -/
structure Operands where
  Val : Type
  Expr : Type
  Ty : Type
  Store : Type
  Prim : Type
  Event : Type
  unitVal : Val
  unitTy : Ty
  isCopy : Ty → Bool
  eval : AList Var Val → Store → Expr → Res (Val × Store)
  life : Life → Store → Store
  test : Val → Option Bool
  allows : Prim → List Val → Store → Answer Val Event Store → Bool

variable {O : Operands} {V : Type}

/-- A Core statement. Every operation that can fail carries its site (for its trap and its
diagnostics). Locals are fresh identities (Q9.1): `bind` introduces a new one, `assign` changes an
existing one. -/
inductive Stmt (O : Operands) where
  /-- Do nothing. -/
  | skip
  /-- Bind a fresh local to an operand's value. -/
  | bind (x : Var) (e : O.Expr) (site : SiteId)
  /-- Store an operand's value in an existing local (the right-hand side first). -/
  | assign (x : Var) (e : O.Expr) (site : SiteId)
  /-- Run `s`, then `t` if `s` completed normally. -/
  | seq (s t : Stmt O)
  /-- A block: a scope for the locals bound in it and the calls deferred in it. -/
  | block (b : BlockId) (s : Stmt O)
  /-- Register a cleanup call in the innermost block; its operands are read when it runs. -/
  | defer (f : FnId) (args : List O.Expr) (site : SiteId)
  /-- Choose a branch by a condition. -/
  | cond (c : O.Expr) (s t : Stmt O) (site : SiteId)
  /-- The one loop form (Q9.3): condition region `c`, test `test`, body `body`. The region's
  locals are visible to the test, then dropped. -/
  | loop (l : LoopId) (c : Stmt O) (test : O.Expr) (body : Stmt O) (site : SiteId)
  /-- Leave loop `l`. -/
  | brk (l : LoopId)
  /-- Start loop `l` again from its condition region. -/
  | cont (l : LoopId)
  /-- Return an operand's value, evaluated before any scope is left. -/
  | ret (e : O.Expr) (site : SiteId)
  /-- Call `f`; bind the result to `dst`, or drop it (a `Unit` call). -/
  | call (dst : Option Var) (f : FnId) (args : List O.Expr) (site : SiteId)
  /-- A primitive operation of the instance (an environment request, or an internal operation such
  as a memory access); bind its result to `dst`, or drop it. -/
  | prim (dst : Option Var) (p : O.Prim) (args : List O.Expr) (site : SiteId)
  /-- The program's own `abort()`: terminal, no cleanup. -/
  | abort (site : SiteId)

/-- A registered cleanup call: the function and its operands, read when it runs. -/
structure Deferred (O : Operands) where
  f : FnId
  args : List O.Expr
  site : SiteId

/-- The statement a deferred call runs: an ordinary call whose `Unit` result is dropped. -/
def Deferred.stmt (d : Deferred O) : Stmt O := .call none d.f d.args d.site

/-- A structured exit in progress. -/
inductive Exit (V : Type) where
  | brk (l : LoopId)
  | cont (l : LoopId)
  | ret (v : V)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- What continues after a cleanup call: the normal end of the block, or an exit. -/
inductive Pending (V : Type) where
  | fall
  | exit (e : Exit V)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A continuation frame: where execution resumes.

- `seq t`: then run `t`.
- `scope b dom defers`: the scope of block `b`. `dom` are the locals visible when it was entered,
  `defers` its registered cleanup calls, newest first.
- `test l c t body site dom`: loop `l`'s condition region is running; then evaluate its test and
  drop the region's locals, keeping those in `dom`, the locals the loop started with.
- `body l c t body site`: loop `l`'s body is running; then start the loop again.
- `resume p`: a cleanup call is running; then continue with `p`.
- `ret dst caller`: a call is running; on its return, restore the caller's locals and bind the
  result to `dst`. -/
inductive Frame (O : Operands) where
  | seq (t : Stmt O)
  | scope (b : BlockId) (dom : List Var) (defers : List (Deferred O))
  | test (l : LoopId) (c : Stmt O) (t : O.Expr) (body : Stmt O) (site : SiteId) (dom : List Var)
  | body (l : LoopId) (c : Stmt O) (t : O.Expr) (body : Stmt O) (site : SiteId)
  | resume (p : Pending O.Val)
  | ret (dst : Option Var) (caller : AList Var O.Val)

/-- What the machine is doing. -/
inductive Ctrl (O : Operands) where
  /-- Run a statement. -/
  | exec (s : Stmt O)
  /-- The current statement completed normally. -/
  | done
  /-- An exit is unwinding the frames. -/
  | exiting (e : Exit O.Val)
  /-- The run trapped: terminal. -/
  | trapped (k : TrapKind) (site : SiteId)

/-- A machine configuration. -/
structure Config (O : Operands) where
  ctrl : Ctrl O
  locals : AList Var O.Val
  store : O.Store
  frames : List (Frame O)

/-- A function: ordinary typed parameters (the first is not special), a result type, the block of
its body, and the body. -/
structure Fn (O : Operands) where
  params : List (Var × O.Ty)
  result : O.Ty
  block : BlockId
  body : Stmt O

/-- A program: its functions, by typed identity. -/
structure Program (O : Operands) where
  fns : AList FnId (Fn O)

/-- How a finished run ended: it completed, it returned a value, or it trapped. -/
inductive Final (V : Type) where
  | normal
  | returned (v : V)
  | trapped (k : TrapKind) (site : SiteId)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The configurations a run ends in. A trap is final whatever frames remain: they are not
unwound. -/
def Config.final? : Config O → Option (Final O.Val)
  | ⟨.done, _, _, []⟩ => some .normal
  | ⟨.exiting (.ret v), _, _, []⟩ => some (.returned v)
  | ⟨.trapped k site, _, _, _⟩ => some (.trapped k site)
  | _ => none

/-- The locals of `L` whose identity is in `dom`: what a scope restores when it is left. -/
def restrict (dom : List Var) (L : AList Var V) : AList Var V :=
  L.filter (fun e => decide (e.1 ∈ dom))

/-- The identities bound in `L`. -/
def dom (L : AList Var V) : List Var := List.map Prod.fst L

/-- Bind a call's or a primitive operation's result to `dst`, a fresh local of the current scope
(as `bind` does), or drop it. -/
def bindDst (dst : Option Var) (v : V) (L : AList Var V) : AList Var V :=
  match dst with
  | none => L
  | some x => (x, v) :: L

/-- The state after `bindDst`: the instrumentation hears of the new local, if there is one. -/
def bindLife (O : Operands) (dst : Option Var) (st : O.Store) : O.Store :=
  match dst with
  | none => st
  | some x => O.life (.bind x) st

/-- The locals of `L` that leaving a scope entered with `dm` drops. -/
def dead (dm : List Var) (L : AList Var V) : List Var := (dom L).filter (fun x => decide (x ∉ dm))

/-- The state after leaving a scope entered with `dm`: the instrumentation hears which locals
went out of scope. -/
def dropOut (O : Operands) (dm : List Var) (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) : O.Store :=
  O.life (.drop (dead dm L)) st

/-- Evaluate arguments left to right, each in the state the one before it left; the first that
traps (or is stuck) decides. -/
def evalArgs (O : Operands) (L : AList Var O.Val) :
    O.Store → List O.Expr → Res (List O.Val × O.Store)
  | st, [] => .ok ([], st)
  | st, e :: es => (O.eval L st e).bind fun (v, st₁) =>
      (evalArgs O L st₁ es).map fun (vs, st₂) => (v :: vs, st₂)

/-- Register a cleanup call in the innermost scope, through the statements that follow it in that
block. A `defer` anywhere else (directly in a loop's condition region, say) has no meaning; the
checker refuses it. -/
def register (d : Deferred O) : List (Frame O) → Option (List (Frame O))
  | .seq t :: k => (register d k).map (.seq t :: ·)
  | .scope b dom ds :: k => some (.scope b dom (d :: ds) :: k)
  | _ => none

/-- What one step does: move to a configuration, or make a request of a primitive operation (its
arguments, its site, the state it is made in, and the configuration each answer leads to). -/
inductive Out (O : Operands) where
  | next (c : Config O)
  | request (p : O.Prim) (args : List O.Val) (site : SiteId) (st : O.Store)
      (k : Answer O.Val O.Event O.Store → Config O)

/-- Continue with an operand's value and the state after it; a trap stops at `site` in the state
before the operand (a trap is terminal, so nothing reads that state's instrumentation again),
stuckness has no step. -/
def onRes (r : Res (V × O.Store)) (site : SiteId) (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store)
    (k : List (Frame O)) (f : V → O.Store → Option (Out O)) : Option (Out O) :=
  match r with
  | .ok (v, st') => f v st'
  | .trap kd => some (.next ⟨.trapped kd site, L, st, k⟩)
  | .stuck => none

/-- Start the next cleanup call of a scope, then continue with `p`. -/
def startCleanup (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) (b : BlockId) (dm : List Var)
    (d : Deferred O) (ds : List (Deferred O)) (p : Pending O.Val) (k : List (Frame O)) : Out O :=
  .next ⟨.exec d.stmt, L, st, .resume p :: .scope b dm ds :: k⟩

/-- One step of an exit `e` unwinding frames `k` with locals `L`: the frame decides, and only a
loop's body frame and a call frame look at the exit itself. -/
def stepExit (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) (e : Exit O.Val) :
    List (Frame O) → Option (Out O)
  | .seq _ :: k => some (.next ⟨.exiting e, L, st, k⟩)
  | .scope b dm (d :: ds) :: k => some (startCleanup L st b dm d ds (.exit e) k)
  | .scope _ dm [] :: k => some (.next ⟨.exiting e, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st, k⟩)
  | .test _ _ _ _ _ dm :: k => some (.next ⟨.exiting e, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st, k⟩)
  | .body l cr t b site :: k => match e with
    | .brk l' =>
      if l' = l then some (.next ⟨.done, L, st, k⟩) else some (.next ⟨.exiting e, L, st, k⟩)
    | .cont l' =>
      if l' = l then some (.next ⟨.exec (.loop l cr t b site), L, st, k⟩)
      else some (.next ⟨.exiting e, L, st, k⟩)
    | .ret _ => some (.next ⟨.exiting e, L, st, k⟩)
  | .ret dst caller :: k => match e with
    | .ret v => some (.next ⟨.done, bindDst dst v caller, bindLife O dst (O.life .leave st), k⟩)
    | _ => none
  | .resume _ :: _ => none
  | [] => none

/-- One step of the machine. `none`: the configuration is final, or no rule applies (unsafe
stuckness). -/
def step (P : Program O) (c : Config O) : Option (Out O) :=
  let L := c.locals
  let st := c.store
  match c.ctrl, c.frames with
  -- running a statement
  | .exec .skip, k => some (.next ⟨.done, L, st, k⟩)
  | .exec (.bind x e site), k => onRes (O.eval L st e) site L st k fun v st' =>
      some (.next ⟨.done, (x, v) :: L, O.life (.bind x) st', k⟩)
  | .exec (.assign x e site), k => onRes (O.eval L st e) site L st k fun v st' =>
      (AList.lookup L x).map fun _ =>
        .next ⟨.done, AList.update L x (fun _ => v), O.life (.bind x) st', k⟩
  | .exec (.seq s t), k => some (.next ⟨.exec s, L, st, .seq t :: k⟩)
  | .exec (.block b s), k => some (.next ⟨.exec s, L, st, .scope b (dom L) [] :: k⟩)
  | .exec (.defer f args site), k =>
      (register ⟨f, args, site⟩ k).map fun k' => .next ⟨.done, L, st, k'⟩
  | .exec (.cond e s t site), k => onRes (O.eval L st e) site L st k fun v st' =>
      (O.test v).map fun b => .next ⟨.exec (if b then s else t), L, st', k⟩
  | .exec (.loop l cr t b site), k =>
      some (.next ⟨.exec cr, L, st, .test l cr t b site (dom L) :: k⟩)
  | .exec (.brk l), k => some (.next ⟨.exiting (.brk l), L, st, k⟩)
  | .exec (.cont l), k => some (.next ⟨.exiting (.cont l), L, st, k⟩)
  | .exec (.ret e site), k => onRes (O.eval L st e) site L st k fun v st' =>
      some (.next ⟨.exiting (.ret v), L, st', k⟩)
  | .exec (.call dst f args site), k => onRes (evalArgs O L st args) site L st k fun vs st' =>
      (AList.lookup P.fns f).bind fun fn =>
        if fn.params.length = vs.length then
          some (.next ⟨.exec fn.body, (fn.params.map Prod.fst).zip vs,
            O.life (.enter (fn.params.map Prod.fst)) st',
            .scope fn.block (fn.params.map Prod.fst) [] :: .ret dst L :: k⟩)
        else none
  | .exec (.prim x p args site), k => onRes (evalArgs O L st args) site L st k fun vs st' =>
      some (.request p vs site st' fun a => match a.result with
        | .ok v => ⟨.done, bindDst x v L, bindLife O x a.store, k⟩
        | .error kd => ⟨.trapped kd site, L, a.store, k⟩)
  | .exec (.abort site), k => some (.next ⟨.trapped .abort site, L, st, k⟩)
  -- a statement completed normally
  | .done, .seq t :: k => some (.next ⟨.exec t, L, st, k⟩)
  | .done, .scope b dm (d :: ds) :: k => some (startCleanup L st b dm d ds .fall k)
  | .done, .scope _ dm [] :: k => some (.next ⟨.done, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st, k⟩)
  | .done, .test l cr t b site dm :: k => onRes (O.eval L st t) site L st k fun v st' =>
      (O.test v).map fun go =>
        if go then .next ⟨.exec b, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st', .body l cr t b site :: k⟩
        else .next ⟨.done, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st', k⟩
  | .done, .body l cr t b site :: k => some (.next ⟨.exec (.loop l cr t b site), L, st, k⟩)
  | .done, .resume .fall :: k => some (.next ⟨.done, L, st, k⟩)
  | .done, .resume (.exit e) :: k => some (.next ⟨.exiting e, L, st, k⟩)
  -- a function body that ends without `return`: only a `Unit` function may (the checker refuses
  -- any other, C9), and its result is `()`
  | .done, .ret dst caller :: k =>
      some (.next ⟨.done, bindDst dst O.unitVal caller, bindLife O dst (O.life .leave st), k⟩)
  | .done, [] => none
  -- an exit unwinding
  | .exiting e, k => stepExit L st e k
  -- a trap is terminal
  | .trapped .., _ => none

/-- A step's label: silent, or an event the environment saw. -/
inductive Label (O : Operands) where
  | silent
  | emit (ev : O.Event)

/-- The label of an answer: its event, if it has one. -/
def Answer.label (a : Answer O.Val O.Event O.Store) : Label O :=
  match a.event with
  | none => .silent
  | some ev => .emit ev

/-- The events a label contributes to a trace. -/
def Label.events : Label O → List O.Event
  | .silent => []
  | .emit ev => [ev]

/-- The labeled single-step relation: a step of the machine itself (silent), or a primitive
operation answered within its contract (labeled with the answer's event, if any). -/
inductive Step (P : Program O) : Config O → Label O → Config O → Prop where
  | silent {c c'} : step P c = some (.next c') → Step P c .silent c'
  | prim {c p vs site st k a} : step P c = some (.request p vs site st k) →
      O.allows p vs st a = true → Step P c a.label (k a)

/-- Many steps, with the events they emitted in order. -/
inductive Steps (P : Program O) : Config O → List O.Event → Config O → Prop where
  | refl (c : Config O) : Steps P c [] c
  | step {c l c' tr c''} : Step P c l c' → Steps P c' tr c'' → Steps P c (l.events ++ tr) c''

/-- The configuration that runs function `fn` on argument values `vs`, in state `st`, as the whole
program: its `return` ends the run with the returned value (`Final.returned`), and a body that ends
without one ends it normally (`Final.normal`). How either becomes the process's exit is C6. -/
def enter (fn : Fn O) (vs : List O.Val) (st : O.Store) : Config O :=
  ⟨.exec fn.body, (fn.params.map Prod.fst).zip vs, O.life (.enter (fn.params.map Prod.fst)) st,
    [.scope fn.block (fn.params.map Prod.fst) []]⟩

/-! ## Cleanup plans

What an exit will run, read from the frames: the frames an exit passes on its way to the frame
that receives it, and the deferred calls of their scopes in the order they run. -/

/-- The frames exit `e` passes through without being received: the rest of a block, a scope, a
loop's condition region, and the body of another loop (or any loop, for a `return`). -/
def Passes (e : Exit O.Val) : Frame O → Prop
  | .seq _ => True
  | .scope .. => True
  | .test .. => True
  | .body l .. => match e with
    | .brk l' => l' ≠ l
    | .cont l' => l' ≠ l
    | .ret _ => True
  | .resume _ => False
  | .ret .. => False

/-- The deferred calls frames hold, in the order an exit through them runs them: the innermost
scope first, and within a scope the newest registration first. -/
def plan : List (Frame O) → List (Deferred O)
  | [] => []
  | .scope _ _ ds :: k => ds ++ plan k
  | _ :: k => plan k

/-- The locals left after an exit passes these frames: each scope restores its own. -/
def unwindLocals : List (Frame O) → AList Var O.Val → AList Var O.Val
  | [], L => L
  | .scope _ dm _ :: k, L => unwindLocals k (restrict dm L)
  | .test _ _ _ _ _ dm :: k, L => unwindLocals k (restrict dm L)
  | _ :: k, L => unwindLocals k L

/-- A cleanup call that completes: from locals `L` and state `st`, on any frames, it runs to its
normal end with events `tr` and state `st'`, and gives the locals back unchanged (a call restores
its caller's locals). -/
def Completes (P : Program O) (L : AList Var O.Val) (d : Deferred O) (st : O.Store)
    (tr : List O.Event) (st' : O.Store) : Prop :=
  ∀ k, Steps P ⟨.exec d.stmt, L, st, k⟩ tr ⟨.done, L, st', k⟩

/-- The calls `ds` run one after another from locals `L`, each completing, the state threaded from
each to the next: in list order, with the events of each in turn. -/
inductive RunsAll (P : Program O) (L : AList Var O.Val) :
    List (Deferred O) → O.Store → List O.Event → O.Store → Prop where
  | nil (st : O.Store) : RunsAll P L [] st [] st
  | cons {d ds st tr₁ st₁ tr₂ st₂} : Completes P L d st tr₁ st₁ → RunsAll P L ds st₁ tr₂ st₂ →
      RunsAll P L (d :: ds) st (tr₁ ++ tr₂) st₂

/-- The cleanup an exit through frames `pre` performs, from locals `L` and state `st`: each scope's
calls run in turn (`RunsAll`), with the locals that scope restores passed on to the next, the
innermost scope first. The calls it runs are exactly `plan pre`, in that order
(`cleans_iff_runs_plan`, `planWith_calls`). -/
inductive Cleans (P : Program O) :
    List (Frame O) → AList Var O.Val → O.Store → List O.Event → O.Store → Prop where
  | nil (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) : Cleans P [] L st [] st
  | scope {b dm ds pre L st tr₁ st₁ tr₂ st₂} : RunsAll P L ds st tr₁ st₁ →
      Cleans P pre (restrict dm L) (dropOut O dm L st₁) tr₂ st₂ →
      Cleans P (.scope b dm ds :: pre) L st (tr₁ ++ tr₂) st₂
  | seq {t pre L st tr st'} : Cleans P pre L st tr st' → Cleans P (.seq t :: pre) L st tr st'
  | test {l cr t b site dm pre L st tr st'} : Cleans P pre (restrict dm L) (dropOut O dm L st) tr st' →
      Cleans P (.test l cr t b site dm :: pre) L st tr st'
  | body {l cr t b site pre L st tr st'} : Cleans P pre L st tr st' →
      Cleans P (.body l cr t b site :: pre) L st tr st'

/-! ## Destruction and receivers -/

/-- The one consuming destruction protocol (OD60, Q2.7, owner decision 2026-11-08 item 7). For each
type with `Destroy`, `impl` names the function that destroys it. That function's only parameter
is an ordinary parameter of that type, taken by value (it consumes the value), and it returns
`Unit`; there is no receiver form. `Copy` and `Destroy` are disjoint (Q2.8). A scope destroys a
value only through a call it registered (`destroyCall`): no destructor runs implicitly. -/
structure DestroyProtocol (P : Program O) where
  impl : O.Ty → Option FnId
  sig : ∀ T f, impl T = some f →
    ∃ fn x, AList.lookup P.fns f = some fn ∧ fn.params = [(x, T)] ∧ fn.result = O.unitTy
  notCopy : ∀ T f, impl T = some f → O.isCopy T = false

/-- The cleanup call that destroys a value of type `T`, given by the operand that moves it. -/
def DestroyProtocol.destroyCall {P : Program O} (D : DestroyProtocol P) (T : O.Ty)
    (arg : O.Expr) (site : SiteId) : Option (Deferred O) :=
  (D.impl T).map fun f => ⟨f, [arg], site⟩

/-! ## Destruction order (Q2.3, Q3.8)

The order in which the container `Destroy` implementations visit the parts of a value. They are
ordinary checked functions (Q2.7); these schedules are what their bodies must follow, stated once
here so every implementation and its proof refer to the same order. A zero-sized element is
visited in its index position like any other (Q3.8): the schedules do not look at sizes. -/

/-- A part of a value that destruction visits. -/
inductive Part where
  /-- Element `i` of a sequence. -/
  | elem (i : Nat)
  /-- The key, or the value, of the entry with the `i`-th smallest key of an ordered map. -/
  | key (i : Nat)
  | value (i : Nat)
  /-- The payload of a heap cell, an `Option` or a `Result`. -/
  | payload
  /-- A sequence's backing storage, a heap cell, or a map's nodes: released last. -/
  | storage
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A sequence of `n` elements: indices `0 … n−1` in increasing order, then the backing (Q2.3). -/
def seqDestroyOrder (n : Nat) : List Part := (List.range n).map .elem ++ [.storage]

/-- An ordered map of `n` entries: ascending key order, each key before its value, then the nodes
(Q2.3). -/
def mapDestroyOrder (n : Nat) : List Part :=
  (List.range n).flatMap (fun i => [.key i, .value i]) ++ [.storage]

/-- A heap cell: its payload, then the cell. -/
def heapDestroyOrder : List Part := [.payload, .storage]

/-- An `Option` or `Result`: the payload that is present, if any. -/
def payloadDestroyOrder (present : Bool) : List Part := if present then [.payload] else []

/-! ## Running

`run` drives the step function with an oracle for the environment's answers, for at most `fuel`
steps. It is the skeleton of the reference evaluator (Q9.9, C8): an answer outside the operation's
contract is refused, never taken, and running out of fuel is reported as such, never as a result
of the program. -/

/-- The answer to a request: given the events so far (oldest first), the operation, its arguments,
its site and the current state. -/
abbrev Oracle (O : Operands) :=
  List O.Event → O.Prim → List O.Val → SiteId → O.Store → Option (Answer O.Val O.Event O.Store)

/-- How a run of `run` ended. -/
inductive Ending (O : Operands) where
  /-- The program finished. -/
  | final (f : Final O.Val)
  /-- No rule applies: unsafe stuckness. -/
  | stuck (c : Config O)
  /-- The oracle gave no answer: the run waits on the environment. C8 refines this (a run that
  blocks forever keeps this classification), and C6 splits a request from its response. -/
  | noAnswer (c : Config O)
  /-- The oracle gave an answer outside the operation's contract: refused, never taken. -/
  | badAnswer (c : Config O)
  /-- The fuel ran out: a statement about the budget only. -/
  | outOfFuel (c : Config O)

/-- A run's events, in order, and how it ended. -/
structure Report (O : Operands) where
  events : List O.Event
  ending : Ending O

/-- Run for at most `fuel` steps; `hist` are the events so far. A final configuration is reported
as final whatever fuel is left, so a run that ends on its last unit of fuel is not "out of fuel". -/
def runFrom (P : Program O) (ρ : Oracle O) : Nat → List O.Event → Config O → Report O
  | fuel, hist, c =>
    match c.final?, fuel with
    | some f, _ => ⟨hist, .final f⟩
    | none, 0 => ⟨hist, .outOfFuel c⟩
    | none, n + 1 =>
      match step P c with
      | none => ⟨hist, .stuck c⟩
      | some (.next c') => runFrom P ρ n hist c'
      | some (.request p vs site st k) =>
        match ρ hist p vs site st with
        | none => ⟨hist, .noAnswer c⟩
        | some a =>
          if O.allows p vs st a then runFrom P ρ n (hist ++ a.event.toList) (k a)
          else ⟨hist, .badAnswer c⟩

/-- Run from `c` with no events yet. -/
def run (P : Program O) (ρ : Oracle O) (fuel : Nat) (c : Config O) : Report O :=
  runFrom P ρ fuel [] c

/-! ## Examples

A small operand language: integer values; literals, locals and a shared counter as operands; the
plain `i32` arithmetic of C2, which traps on failure; one operand that traps with a given kind (to
show which failure is reported first); and two primitives:
`log`, which emits its argument as an event, and `bump`, an internal operation that adds one to the
counter without an event. -/

namespace Demo

inductive E where
  | lit (n : Int)
  | var (x : Var)
  | counter
  | fail (k : TrapKind)
  /-- `move x`: the value of `x`, which the instrumentation then records as moved. -/
  | move (x : Var)
  /-- Plain `i32` arithmetic (C2): a failure is a trap of its kind. -/
  | arith (op : ArithOp) (a b : E)

/-- The types: only `Unit` and integers matter here. -/
inductive T where
  | unit
  | int
  /-- `Tok`, a non-`Copy` type holding an integer: the type the `move` examples move (Q9.1 allows
  `move` only on a non-`Copy` type). The demo machine does not check types, so `log` takes the
  integer a `Tok` holds. -/
  | tok
  deriving DecidableEq

/-- The demo's primitives. -/
inductive Pr where
  | log
  | bump

/-- The demo's shared state: a counter, and a toy ownership instrumentation (C5 defines the real
one): for each activation, innermost first, the locals moved and not reinitialized since. -/
structure DState where
  counter : Int
  moved : List (List Var)
  deriving DecidableEq

/-- The state a run starts in. -/
def DState.init : DState := ⟨0, []⟩

/-- The locals moved in the current activation. -/
def DState.top (st : DState) : List Var := st.moved.headD []

/-- Change the current activation's moved locals. -/
def DState.onTop (f : List Var → List Var) (st : DState) : DState :=
  { st with moved := match st.moved with
    | [] => []
    | m :: rest => f m :: rest }

/-- The toy instrumentation's life: a new activation starts with nothing moved, an initialized
local is not moved, a local out of scope is forgotten. -/
def DState.life : Life → DState → DState
  | .bind x, st => st.onTop (·.filter (· ≠ x))
  | .enter _, st => { st with moved := [] :: st.moved }
  | .leave, st => { st with moved := st.moved.tail }
  | .drop xs, st => st.onTop (·.filter (· ∉ xs))

/-- Evaluate an operand. Only `move` changes the state, and only the instrumentation: reading a
moved local is stuck (the checker refuses such a program, C5). A failing operation traps, an
unbound local is stuck. -/
def evalE (L : AList Var Int) (st : DState) : E → Res (Int × DState)
  | .lit n => .ok (n, st)
  | .var x => match AList.lookup L x with
    | some v => if x ∈ st.top then .stuck else .ok (v, st)
    | none => .stuck
  | .move x => match AList.lookup L x with
    | some v => if x ∈ st.top then .stuck else .ok (v, st.onTop (x :: ·))
    | none => .stuck
  | .counter => .ok (st.counter, st)
  | .fail k => .trap k
  | .arith op a b => (evalE L st a).bind fun (x, st₁) => (evalE L st₁ b).bind fun (y, st₂) =>
      (plain .i32 op x y).map fun r => (r, st₂)

abbrev ops : Operands where
  Val := Int
  Expr := E
  Ty := T
  Store := DState
  Prim := Pr
  Event := Int
  unitVal := 0
  unitTy := .unit
  isCopy := fun t => t == .int
  eval := evalE
  life := DState.life
  test := fun v => some (v ≠ 0)
  allows := fun p vs st a => match p, vs, a.event, a.result with
    | .log, [v], some ev, .ok 0 => decide (ev = v) && decide (a.store = st)
    | .bump, [], none, .ok 0 => decide (a.store = { st with counter := st.counter + 1 })
    | _, _, _, _ => false

/-- The environment logs every `log` argument; `bump` adds one to the counter. -/
def env : Oracle ops := fun _ p vs _ st => match p, vs with
  | .log, [v] => some ⟨some v, .ok 0, st⟩
  | .bump, [] => some ⟨none, .ok 0, { st with counter := st.counter + 1 }⟩
  | _, _ => none

def x : Var := ⟨"x", 0⟩
def y : Var := ⟨"y", 1⟩
def a : Var := ⟨"a", 2⟩
def t : Var := ⟨"t", 3⟩
def p : Var := ⟨"p", 4⟩
def s : SiteId := ⟨0⟩
def logF : FnId := ⟨⟨[]⟩, "log"⟩
def boomF : FnId := ⟨⟨[]⟩, "boom"⟩
def mainF : FnId := ⟨⟨[]⟩, "main"⟩

/-- `fn log(x: Int) -> Unit { log x }` -/
def logFn : Fn ops := ⟨[(x, .int)], .unit, ⟨100⟩, .prim none .log [.var x] s⟩

/-- `fn boom() -> Unit { abort() }` -/
def boomFn : Fn ops := ⟨[], .unit, ⟨101⟩, .abort ⟨9⟩⟩

def bumpF : FnId := ⟨⟨[]⟩, "bump"⟩

/-- `fn bump() -> Unit { bump }` -/
def bumpFn : Fn ops := ⟨[], .unit, ⟨102⟩, .prim none .bump [] s⟩

def eatF : FnId := ⟨⟨[]⟩, "eat"⟩

/-- `fn eat(p: Tok) -> Unit { log(move p) }` -/
def eatFn : Fn ops := ⟨[(p, .tok)], .unit, ⟨103⟩, .prim none .log [.move p] s⟩

def prog : Program ops := ⟨[(logF, logFn), (boomF, boomFn), (bumpF, bumpFn), (eatF, eatFn)]⟩

def dlog (n : Int) : Stmt ops := .defer logF [.lit n] s
def callLog (n : Int) : Stmt ops := .call none logF [.lit n] s

/-- Run a body as `main`. -/
def go (body : Stmt ops) : Report ops := run prog env 200 (enter ⟨[], .int, ⟨0⟩, body⟩ [] .init)

/-- A report's events and, if the program finished, how. -/
def brief (r : Report ops) : List Int × Option (Final Int) :=
  (r.events, match r.ending with
    | .final f => some f
    | _ => none)

/-- The report says the run was stuck. -/
def isStuck (r : Report ops) : Bool := match r.ending with
  | .stuck _ => true
  | _ => false

/-- The report says the fuel ran out. -/
def isOutOfFuel (r : Report ops) : Bool := match r.ending with
  | .outOfFuel _ => true
  | _ => false

/-- Normal block exit runs the innermost scope's calls first, and the newest registration of each
scope first:
```
{ defer log(1); defer log(2); { defer log(3); } } log(4);
```
logs 3, 2, 1, then 4. -/
example : (go (.seq (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (dlog 1) (.seq (dlog 2) (.block ⟨2⟩ (dlog 3)))))
    (callLog 4))).events = [3, 2, 1, 4] := by decide

/-- `return` evaluates its value first, then runs the cleanup of every scope it leaves, and the
value comes out unchanged:
```
{ defer log(1); { defer log(2); return 7; } }
```
logs 2, 1 and returns 7. -/
example : brief (go (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (dlog 1) (.block ⟨2⟩ (.seq (dlog 2) (.ret (.lit 7) s)))))) =
    ([2, 1], some (.returned 7)) := by decide

/-- `abort()` runs no deferred call:
```
{ defer log(1); abort(); }
```
logs nothing and traps with `abort`. -/
example : brief (go (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (dlog 1) (.abort ⟨5⟩)))) = ([], some (.trapped .abort ⟨5⟩)) := by
  decide

/-- A cleanup call that traps stops the run: no remaining cleanup runs.
```
{ defer log(1); defer boom(); }
```
`boom` runs first (the newest registration) and aborts, so `log(1)` never runs. -/
example : brief (go (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (dlog 1) (.defer boomF [] s)))) =
    ([], some (.trapped .abort ⟨9⟩)) := by decide

/-- `break` runs the cleanup of the scopes it leaves, then continues after the loop:
```
while 'l { } test 1 do { defer log(5); break 'l; } log(6);
```
logs 5, then 6. -/
example : (go (.seq (.loop ⟨0⟩ .skip (.lit 1) (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (dlog 5) (.brk ⟨0⟩))) s)
    (callLog 6))).events = [5, 6] := by decide

/-- A deferred call reads its operands when it runs (late reads, Q9.8):
```
let x = 1; { defer log(x); x = 2; }
```
logs 2. -/
example : (go (.seq (.bind x (.lit 1) s)
    (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (.defer logF [.var x] s) (.assign x (.lit 2) s))))).events = [2] := by
  decide

/-- Arguments are evaluated left to right; the first that traps decides the trap. -/
example : brief (go (.call none logF [.fail .overflow, .fail .divisionByZero] ⟨3⟩)) =
    ([], some (.trapped .overflow ⟨3⟩)) := by decide

/-- A block's locals are gone after it; an assignment to an outer local stays:
```
let x = 1; { let y = 5; x = 2; } return x;
```
returns 2; reading `y` after the block is stuck. -/
example : brief (go (.seq (.bind x (.lit 1) s)
    (.seq (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (.bind y (.lit 5) s) (.assign x (.lit 2) s))) (.ret (.var x) s)))) =
    ([], some (.returned 2)) := by decide

example : isStuck (go (.seq (.block ⟨1⟩ (.bind y (.lit 5) s)) (.ret (.var y) s))) = true := by
  decide

/-- The shared state is not restored on return: what a cleanup call does to it is seen after the
block.
```
{ defer bump(); defer bump(); } log(counter);
```
logs 2. -/
example : (go (.seq (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (.defer bumpF [] s) (.defer bumpF [] s)))
    (.prim none .log [.counter] s))).events = [2] := by decide

/-- Plain arithmetic of C2 runs inside the machine: `log(2147483647 + 1)` traps with kind
`overflow` at its site, and `log(7 / 2)` logs 3. -/
example : brief (go (.prim none .log [.arith .add (.lit 2147483647) (.lit 1)] ⟨4⟩)) =
    ([], some (.trapped .overflow ⟨4⟩)) := by decide

example : (go (.prim none .log [.arith .div (.lit 7) (.lit 2)] s)).events = [3] := by decide

/-- A cleanup call whose argument overflows traps when it runs, after the newer calls ran:
```
{ defer log(2147483647 + 1); defer log(1); }
```
logs 1, then traps with `overflow`. -/
example : brief (go (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (.defer logF [.arith .add (.lit 2147483647) (.lit 1)] ⟨6⟩)
    (dlog 1)))) = ([1], some (.trapped .overflow ⟨6⟩)) := by decide

/-- A run that is final on its last unit of fuel is final, not out of fuel. -/
example : brief (run prog env 0 ⟨.done, [], .init, []⟩) = ([], some .normal) := by decide

/-- An oracle that does not answer leaves the run waiting, which is not a refused answer; an answer
outside the contract is refused. -/
example : (match (run prog (fun _ _ _ _ _ => none) 10 (enter ⟨[], .int, ⟨0⟩, callLog 1⟩ [] .init)).ending with
    | .noAnswer _ => true
    | _ => false) = true := by decide

example : (match (run prog (fun _ _ _ _ st => some ⟨some 5, .ok 0, st⟩) 10
    (enter ⟨[], .int, ⟨0⟩, callLog 1⟩ [] .init)).ending with
    | .badAnswer _ => true
    | _ => false) = true := by decide

/-- Operands thread the instrumentation, left to right. Moving a local into a call transfers it:
the caller cannot read it afterwards, and a second move of it in the same argument list already
sees the first.
```
let a: Tok = 7; log(move a); return a;   // logs 7, then reading a is stuck
let a: Tok = 7; log(move a + move a);    // stuck before the call: a is moved by the first operand
```
-/
example : (fun r : Report ops => (r.events, isStuck r)) (go (.seq (.bind a (.lit 7) s)
    (.seq (.call none logF [.move a] s) (.ret (.var a) s)))) = ([7], true) := by decide

example : (fun r : Report ops => (r.events, isStuck r)) (go (.seq (.bind a (.lit 7) s)
    (.call none logF [.arith .add (.move a) (.move a)] s))) = ([], true) := by decide

/-- Assigning a moved local reinitializes it; a function that moves its parameter can be called
again, each activation having its own instrumentation.
```
let a: Tok = 7; log(move a); a = 8; log(move a);   // logs 7, 8
fn eat(p: Tok) { log(move p) }   eat(1); eat(2);    // logs 1, 2
```
-/
example : (go (.seq (.bind a (.lit 7) s) (.seq (.call none logF [.move a] s)
    (.seq (.assign a (.lit 8) s) (.call none logF [.move a] s))))).events = [7, 8] := by decide

example : brief (go (.seq (.call none eatF [.lit 1] s) (.call none eatF [.lit 2] s))) =
    ([1, 2], some .normal) := by decide

/-- A condition region's temporaries are visible to the test and gone after it:
```
while 'l { bump(); let t = counter - 3; } test t do { log(counter); continue 'l; }
log(counter);
```
logs 1, 2 (the test is `t ≠ 0`), then 3; reading `t` after the loop is stuck. -/
example : brief (go (.seq (.loop ⟨0⟩ (.seq (.call none bumpF [] s) (.bind t (.arith .sub .counter
    (.lit 3)) s)) (.var t) (.block ⟨1⟩ (.seq (.prim none .log [.counter] s) (.cont ⟨0⟩))) s)
    (.prim none .log [.counter] s))) = ([1, 2, 3], some .normal) := by decide

example : isStuck (go (.seq (.loop ⟨0⟩ (.bind t (.lit 0) s) (.var t) .skip s) (.ret (.var t) s)))
    = true := by decide

/-- Running out of fuel is reported as such: a loop that never ends is not a result. -/
example : isOutOfFuel (run prog env 50 (enter ⟨[], .int, ⟨0⟩, .loop ⟨0⟩ .skip (.lit 1) .skip s⟩ [] .init))
    = true := by decide

end Demo

end ConcreteSpec.Core.Exec
import ConcreteSpec.Spec.Exec

/-!
# The laws of execution, calls and cleanup (`Spec/Exec.lean`, foundation contract C1)

PROPOSED: not normative until the owner freezes Core (ROADMAP A2c). See `ConcreteSpec/README.md`.

What these prove, for every operand language:

- **Sequencing**: `seq s t` runs `s` with `t` as its continuation; `t` runs when `s` completes, and
  an exit from `s` skips it (`step_seq`, `step_done_seq`, `step_exit_seq`).
- **Scope restoration**: leaving a scope keeps exactly the locals visible when it was entered,
  with their current values (`lookup_restrict`); the shared state is not restored: a call's
  return keeps the state the callee left (`step_return_caller`).
- **Registration**: `defer` adds a call as the newest of the innermost scope (`register_scope`),
  so `defer d₁; defer d₂` leaves `d₂` before `d₁` (`two_defers_register`).
- **Cleanup order**, in two forms. *Every* run: the only step an exit or the end of a block can
  take at a scope with registered calls starts its newest one (`exit_scope_next`,
  `done_scope_next`). *A run whose calls complete*: an exit through a scope runs its calls newest
  first, threading the state, and then restores the scope (`exit_scope_runs_defers`,
  `done_scope_runs_defers`); an exit through several frames runs the calls of `plan`, innermost
  scope first, and arrives unchanged (`unwind_runs_plan`); the cleanup runs exactly the calls of
  `plan`, in order (`cleans_iff_runs_plan`, `planWith_calls`); a `return` then reaches its caller with
  the value bound (`return_reaches_caller`), and a `break` or `continue` its loop
  (`break_reaches_loop`, `continue_reaches_loop`).
- **Exit values**: a `return` evaluates its value before any cleanup (`step_ret`).
- **Traps are terminal**: a trapped configuration has no step and no later event
  (`steps_from_trapped`); `abort()` runs no cleanup (`abort_runs_no_cleanup`), and so neither does a
  cleanup call that traps.
- **Evaluation order**: the first argument that traps decides the trap (`evalArgs_first_trap`).
- Steps of the machine itself are deterministic (`step_silent_deterministic`): only the answers of
  primitive operations, each allowed by its contract, can make two runs differ
  (`step_deterministic`).
- **Destruction order** (Q2.3): a sequence's element `i` is visited `i`-th and the backing last; a
  map's `i`-th entry has its key at `2i` and its value at `2i+1`, and the nodes come last.
-/

namespace ConcreteSpec.Core.Exec
open ConcreteSpec.Core

variable {O : Operands} {V : Type}

/-! ### Many steps -/

theorem Steps.single {P : Program O} {c c' : Config O} {l : Label O} (h : Step P c l c') :
    Steps P c l.events c' := by
  have := Steps.step h (Steps.refl c')
  simpa using this

theorem Steps.trans {P : Program O} {c c' c'' : Config O} {tr tr' : List O.Event}
    (h : Steps P c tr c') (h' : Steps P c' tr' c'') : Steps P c (tr ++ tr') c'' := by
  induction h with
  | refl => simpa using h'
  | step hs _ ih => simpa [List.append_assoc] using Steps.step hs (ih h')

/-- A silent step followed by more steps. -/
theorem steps_next {P : Program O} {c c' c'' : Config O} {tr : List O.Event}
    (h : step P c = some (.next c')) (h' : Steps P c' tr c'') : Steps P c tr c'' := by
  simpa [Label.events] using Steps.step (Step.silent h) h'

/-- A primitive operation answered within its contract, followed by more steps. -/
theorem steps_request {P : Program O} {c c'' : Config O} {p : O.Prim} {vs : List O.Val}
    {site : SiteId} {st : O.Store} {k : Answer O.Val O.Event O.Store → Config O}
    {a : Answer O.Val O.Event O.Store} {tr : List O.Event}
    (h : step P c = some (.request p vs site st k)) (ha : O.allows p vs st a = true)
    (h' : Steps P (k a) tr c'') : Steps P c (a.label.events ++ tr) c'' :=
  Steps.step (Step.prim h ha) h'

/-! ### Sequencing -/

theorem step_seq (P : Program O) (s t : Stmt O) (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store)
    (k : List (Frame O)) :
    step P ⟨.exec (.seq s t), L, st, k⟩ = some (.next ⟨.exec s, L, st, .seq t :: k⟩) := rfl

theorem step_done_seq (P : Program O) (t : Stmt O) (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store)
    (k : List (Frame O)) :
    step P ⟨.done, L, st, .seq t :: k⟩ = some (.next ⟨.exec t, L, st, k⟩) := rfl

theorem step_exit_seq (P : Program O) (e : Exit O.Val) (t : Stmt O) (L : AList Var O.Val)
    (st : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) :
    step P ⟨.exiting e, L, st, .seq t :: k⟩ = some (.next ⟨.exiting e, L, st, k⟩) := rfl

/-- Entering a block records the locals visible at its start. -/
theorem step_block (P : Program O) (b : BlockId) (s : Stmt O) (L : AList Var O.Val)
    (st : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) :
    step P ⟨.exec (.block b s), L, st, k⟩ =
      some (.next ⟨.exec s, L, st, .scope b (dom L) [] :: k⟩) := rfl

/-! ### Scope restoration -/

theorem lookup_restrict (dm : List Var) : ∀ (L : AList Var V) (x : Var),
    AList.lookup (restrict dm L) x = if x ∈ dm then AList.lookup L x else none
  | [], x => by by_cases h : x ∈ dm <;> simp [restrict, AList.lookup, h]
  | (y, v) :: L, x => by
    have ih := lookup_restrict dm L x
    by_cases hy : y ∈ dm
    · have hr : restrict dm ((y, v) :: L) = (y, v) :: restrict dm L := by
        simp [restrict, hy]
      rw [hr]
      by_cases hyx : y = x
      · subst hyx; simp [AList.lookup, hy]
      · simp only [AList.lookup, ite_eq_right hyx]; exact ih
    · have hr : restrict dm ((y, v) :: L) = restrict dm L := by
        simp [restrict, hy]
      rw [hr, ih]
      by_cases hyx : y = x
      · subst hyx; simp [hy]
      · simp [AList.lookup, hyx]

/-- Leaving a scope entered with locals `L₀` keeps no local bound inside it. -/
theorem restrict_dom_drops (L₀ L : AList Var V) (x : Var) (hx : x ∉ dom L₀) :
    AList.lookup (restrict (dom L₀) L) x = none := by
  rw [lookup_restrict]; simp [hx]

/-! ### Registration -/

/-- A deferred call is registered as the newest call of the innermost scope. -/
theorem register_scope (d : Deferred O) (b : BlockId) (dm : List Var) (ds : List (Deferred O))
    (k : List (Frame O)) : register d (.scope b dm ds :: k) = some (.scope b dm (d :: ds) :: k) :=
  rfl

theorem register_seq (d : Deferred O) (t : Stmt O) (k : List (Frame O)) :
    register d (.seq t :: k) = (register d k).map (.seq t :: ·) := rfl

/-- **Reverse registration order.** A block running `defer d₁; defer d₂; s` reaches `s` with its
scope holding `d₂` before `d₁`, so `d₂` runs first; nothing is emitted. -/
theorem two_defers_register (P : Program O) (b : BlockId) (d₁ d₂ : Deferred O) (s : Stmt O)
    (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) :
    Steps P ⟨.exec (.block b (.seq (.defer d₁.f d₁.args d₁.site)
        (.seq (.defer d₂.f d₂.args d₂.site) s))), L, st, k⟩ []
      ⟨.exec s, L, st, .scope b (dom L) [d₂, d₁] :: k⟩ :=
  steps_next rfl (steps_next rfl (steps_next rfl (steps_next rfl (steps_next rfl
    (steps_next rfl (steps_next rfl (Steps.refl _)))))))

/-! ### Exits -/

/-- `return` evaluates its value first: the next configuration carries the value and the same
frames, so no cleanup has run. -/
theorem step_ret (P : Program O) (e : O.Expr) (site : SiteId) (L : AList Var O.Val)
    (st st' : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) (v : O.Val) (hv : O.eval L st e = .ok (v, st')) :
    step P ⟨.exec (.ret e site), L, st, k⟩ = some (.next ⟨.exiting (.ret v), L, st', k⟩) := by
  simp [step, onRes, hv]

/-- A `break` reaching its loop's body frame continues after the loop. -/
theorem step_break_loop (P : Program O) (l : LoopId) (cr b : Stmt O) (t : O.Expr) (site : SiteId)
    (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) :
    step P ⟨.exiting (.brk l), L, st, .body l cr t b site :: k⟩ =
      some (.next ⟨.done, L, st, k⟩) := by
  simp [step, stepExit]

/-- A `continue` reaching its loop's body frame starts the loop again from its condition region. -/
theorem step_continue_loop (P : Program O) (l : LoopId) (cr b : Stmt O) (t : O.Expr)
    (site : SiteId) (L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) :
    step P ⟨.exiting (.cont l), L, st, .body l cr t b site :: k⟩ =
      some (.next ⟨.exec (.loop l cr t b site), L, st, k⟩) := by
  simp [step, stepExit]

/-- A `return` reaching the call frame restores the caller's locals and binds the value; the state
is the one the callee left, with the instrumentation told the activation ended and the result
bound. -/
theorem step_return_caller (P : Program O) (v : O.Val) (dst : Option Var)
    (caller L : AList Var O.Val) (st : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) :
    step P ⟨.exiting (.ret v), L, st, .ret dst caller :: k⟩ =
      some (.next ⟨.done, bindDst dst v caller, bindLife O dst (O.life .leave st), k⟩) := rfl

/-! ### Cleanup -/

/-- **Every run.** At a scope with registered calls, the only step an exit can take starts the
newest call, remembering the exit. -/
theorem exit_scope_next {P : Program O} {e : Exit O.Val} {b : BlockId} {dm : List Var}
    {d : Deferred O} {ds : List (Deferred O)} {L : AList Var O.Val} {st : O.Store}
    {k : List (Frame O)} {l : Label O} {c : Config O}
    (h : Step P ⟨.exiting e, L, st, .scope b dm (d :: ds) :: k⟩ l c) :
    l = .silent ∧ c = ⟨.exec d.stmt, L, st, .resume (.exit e) :: .scope b dm ds :: k⟩ := by
  cases h with
  | silent h =>
    simp only [step, stepExit, startCleanup, Option.some.injEq, Out.next.injEq] at h
    exact ⟨rfl, h.symm⟩
  | prim h _ => simp [step, stepExit, startCleanup] at h

/-- The same at the normal end of a block. -/
theorem done_scope_next {P : Program O} {b : BlockId} {dm : List Var} {d : Deferred O}
    {ds : List (Deferred O)} {L : AList Var O.Val} {st : O.Store} {k : List (Frame O)}
    {l : Label O} {c : Config O} (h : Step P ⟨.done, L, st, .scope b dm (d :: ds) :: k⟩ l c) :
    l = .silent ∧ c = ⟨.exec d.stmt, L, st, .resume .fall :: .scope b dm ds :: k⟩ := by
  cases h with
  | silent h =>
    simp only [step, startCleanup, Option.some.injEq, Out.next.injEq] at h
    exact ⟨rfl, h.symm⟩
  | prim h _ => simp [step, startCleanup] at h

/-- An exit leaving a scope whose calls complete runs them newest first, each with its events and
its effect on the state, and leaves the scope with its locals restored; the exit, and a returned
value, are unchanged. -/
theorem exit_scope_runs_defers (P : Program O) (e : Exit O.Val) (b : BlockId) (dm : List Var)
    (L : AList Var O.Val) (k : List (Frame O)) {ds : List (Deferred O)} {st st' : O.Store}
    {tr : List O.Event} (h : RunsAll P L ds st tr st') :
    Steps P ⟨.exiting e, L, st, .scope b dm ds :: k⟩ tr
      ⟨.exiting e, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st', k⟩ := by
  induction h with
  | nil st => exact steps_next rfl (Steps.refl _)
  | @cons d ds st tr₁ st₁ tr₂ st₂ hd _ ih =>
    exact steps_next (c := ⟨.exiting e, L, st, .scope b dm (d :: ds) :: k⟩) rfl
      (Steps.trans (hd _) (steps_next rfl ih))

/-- The normal end of a block runs its calls the same way. -/
theorem done_scope_runs_defers (P : Program O) (b : BlockId) (dm : List Var)
    (L : AList Var O.Val) (k : List (Frame O)) {ds : List (Deferred O)} {st st' : O.Store}
    {tr : List O.Event} (h : RunsAll P L ds st tr st') :
    Steps P ⟨.done, L, st, .scope b dm ds :: k⟩ tr ⟨.done, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st', k⟩ := by
  induction h with
  | nil st => exact steps_next rfl (Steps.refl _)
  | @cons d ds st tr₁ st₁ tr₂ st₂ hd _ ih =>
    exact steps_next (c := ⟨.done, L, st, .scope b dm (d :: ds) :: k⟩) rfl
      (Steps.trans (hd _) (steps_next rfl ih))

/-- **Cleanup order.** When the cleanup calls complete (`Cleans`, which runs the calls of
`plan pre` in that order: the innermost scope first, each scope's newest registration first), an
exit passing frames `pre` arrives at the frames below with the same exit, the locals the scopes
restored and the state the calls left. -/
theorem unwind_runs_plan (P : Program O) (e : Exit O.Val) (k : List (Frame O))
    {pre : List (Frame O)} {L : AList Var O.Val} {st st' : O.Store} {tr : List O.Event}
    (h : Cleans P pre L st tr st') (hp : ∀ f ∈ pre, Passes e f) :
    Steps P ⟨.exiting e, L, st, pre ++ k⟩ tr ⟨.exiting e, unwindLocals pre L, st', k⟩ := by
  induction h with
  | nil L st => exact Steps.refl _
  | @scope b dm ds pre L st tr₁ st₁ tr₂ st₂ hr _ ih =>
    have h1 := exit_scope_runs_defers P e b dm L (pre ++ k) hr
    have h2 := ih (fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf))
    simpa [unwindLocals] using Steps.trans h1 h2
  | seq _ ih => exact steps_next rfl (ih (fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf)))
  | test _ ih => exact steps_next rfl (ih (fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf)))
  | @body l cr t b site pre L st tr st' _ ih =>
    have ih' := ih (fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf))
    have hf : Passes e (.body l cr t b site) := hp _ List.mem_cons_self
    cases e with
    | brk l' =>
      have hne : l' ≠ l := hf
      exact steps_next (by simp [step, stepExit, hne]) ih'
    | cont l' =>
      have hne : l' ≠ l := hf
      exact steps_next (by simp [step, stepExit, hne]) ih'
    | ret v => exact steps_next rfl ih'

/-- The plan of nested scopes lists the inner scope's calls first. -/
theorem plan_scopes (b₁ b₂ : BlockId) (d₁ d₂ : List Var) (ds₁ ds₂ : List (Deferred O))
    (k : List (Frame O)) :
    plan (.scope b₁ d₁ ds₁ :: .scope b₂ d₂ ds₂ :: k) = ds₁ ++ (ds₂ ++ plan k) := rfl

/-- **A `return` reaches its caller** after the cleanup of every scope it leaves: the caller's
locals come back with the value bound, in the state the cleanup left. -/
theorem return_reaches_caller (P : Program O) (v : O.Val) (dst : Option Var)
    (caller : AList Var O.Val) (k : List (Frame O)) {pre : List (Frame O)}
    {L : AList Var O.Val} {st st' : O.Store} {tr : List O.Event}
    (h : Cleans P pre L st tr st') (hp : ∀ f ∈ pre, Passes (.ret v) f) :
    Steps P ⟨.exiting (.ret v), L, st, pre ++ .ret dst caller :: k⟩ tr
      ⟨.done, bindDst dst v caller, bindLife O dst (O.life .leave st'), k⟩ := by
  have := Steps.trans (unwind_runs_plan P (.ret v) (.ret dst caller :: k) h hp)
    (steps_next rfl (Steps.refl _))
  simpa using this

/-- **A `break` reaches its loop** after the cleanup of the scopes inside the loop body, and the
loop is done. -/
theorem break_reaches_loop (P : Program O) (l : LoopId) (cr b : Stmt O) (t : O.Expr)
    (site : SiteId) (k : List (Frame O)) {pre : List (Frame O)} {L : AList Var O.Val}
    {st st' : O.Store} {tr : List O.Event}
    (h : Cleans P pre L st tr st') (hp : ∀ f ∈ pre, Passes (.brk l) f) :
    Steps P ⟨.exiting (.brk l), L, st, pre ++ .body l cr t b site :: k⟩ tr
      ⟨.done, unwindLocals pre L, st', k⟩ := by
  have := Steps.trans (unwind_runs_plan P (.brk l) (.body l cr t b site :: k) h hp)
    (steps_next (step_break_loop P l cr b t site _ st' k) (Steps.refl _))
  simpa using this

/-- **A `continue` reaches its loop** after the same cleanup, and the loop starts again from its
condition region. -/
theorem continue_reaches_loop (P : Program O) (l : LoopId) (cr b : Stmt O) (t : O.Expr)
    (site : SiteId) (k : List (Frame O)) {pre : List (Frame O)} {L : AList Var O.Val}
    {st st' : O.Store} {tr : List O.Event}
    (h : Cleans P pre L st tr st') (hp : ∀ f ∈ pre, Passes (.cont l) f) :
    Steps P ⟨.exiting (.cont l), L, st, pre ++ .body l cr t b site :: k⟩ tr
      ⟨.exec (.loop l cr t b site), unwindLocals pre L, st', k⟩ := by
  have := Steps.trans (unwind_runs_plan P (.cont l) (.body l cr t b site :: k) h hp)
    (steps_next (step_continue_loop P l cr b t site _ st' k) (Steps.refl _))
  simpa using this

/-! ### What the cleanup runs

`Cleans` runs the calls of `plan`, each with the locals of its scope's point in the unwinding, and
tells the instrumentation which locals each scope it leaves drops: `planWith` lists those actions in
order, its calls are `plan`'s (`planWith_calls`), and a cleanup is a run of exactly those actions
(`cleans_iff_runs_plan`). -/

/-- One action of a cleanup: a call, with the locals it runs with, or leaving a scope entered with
`dm`, from locals `L`. -/
inductive CleanAct (O : Operands) where
  | call (L : AList Var O.Val) (d : Deferred O)
  | drop (dm : List Var) (L : AList Var O.Val)

/-- The call of an action, if it is one. -/
def CleanAct.call? : CleanAct O → Option (Deferred O)
  | .call _ d => some d
  | .drop .. => none

/-- The actions an exit through frames `pre` performs, from locals `L`, in order. -/
def planWith : List (Frame O) → AList Var O.Val → List (CleanAct O)
  | [], _ => []
  | .scope _ dm ds :: k, L => ds.map (.call L) ++ .drop dm L :: planWith k (restrict dm L)
  | .test _ _ _ _ _ dm :: k, L => .drop dm L :: planWith k (restrict dm L)
  | _ :: k, L => planWith k L

theorem planWith_calls : ∀ (pre : List (Frame O)) (L : AList Var O.Val),
    (planWith pre L).filterMap CleanAct.call? = plan pre
  | [], _ => rfl
  | .scope _ dm ds :: k, L => by
    have hds : (ds.map (CleanAct.call L)).filterMap CleanAct.call? = ds := by
      induction ds with
      | nil => rfl
      | cons d ds ih => simp [CleanAct.call?] at ih ⊢; exact ih
    simp only [planWith, plan, List.filterMap_append, hds, List.filterMap_cons, CleanAct.call?,
      planWith_calls k (restrict dm L)]
  | .test _ _ _ _ _ dm :: k, L => by
    simp only [planWith, plan, List.filterMap_cons, CleanAct.call?]
    exact planWith_calls k (restrict dm L)
  | .seq _ :: k, L => planWith_calls k L
  | .body .. :: k, L => planWith_calls k L
  | .resume _ :: k, L => planWith_calls k L
  | .ret .. :: k, L => planWith_calls k L

/-- A list of cleanup actions run one after another, the state threaded: each call completes, and
each scope left tells the instrumentation what it drops. -/
inductive RunsEach (P : Program O) : List (CleanAct O) → O.Store → List O.Event → O.Store → Prop where
  | nil (st : O.Store) : RunsEach P [] st [] st
  | call {L d rest st tr₁ st₁ tr₂ st₂} : Completes P L d st tr₁ st₁ →
      RunsEach P rest st₁ tr₂ st₂ → RunsEach P (.call L d :: rest) st (tr₁ ++ tr₂) st₂
  | drop {dm L rest st tr st'} : RunsEach P rest (dropOut O dm L st) tr st' →
      RunsEach P (.drop dm L :: rest) st tr st'

theorem RunsAll.each {P : Program O} {L : AList Var O.Val} {ds : List (Deferred O)}
    {st st₁ st₂ : O.Store} {tr₁ tr₂ : List O.Event} {rest : List (CleanAct O)}
    (h : RunsAll P L ds st tr₁ st₁) (h' : RunsEach P rest st₁ tr₂ st₂) :
    RunsEach P (ds.map (.call L) ++ rest) st (tr₁ ++ tr₂) st₂ := by
  induction h with
  | nil => simpa using h'
  | cons hd _ ih => simpa [List.append_assoc] using RunsEach.call hd (ih h')

theorem RunsEach.split_calls {P : Program O} {L : AList Var O.Val} :
    ∀ {ds : List (Deferred O)} {rest : List (CleanAct O)} {st st₂ : O.Store} {tr : List O.Event},
    RunsEach P (ds.map (.call L) ++ rest) st tr st₂ →
    ∃ tr₁ st₁ tr₂, tr = tr₁ ++ tr₂ ∧ RunsAll P L ds st tr₁ st₁ ∧ RunsEach P rest st₁ tr₂ st₂
  | [], _, _, _, _, h => ⟨[], _, _, rfl, .nil _, by simpa using h⟩
  | d :: ds, rest, st, st₂, tr, h => by
    cases h with
    | call hd hr =>
      obtain ⟨tr₁, st₁, tr₂, rfl, ha, hb⟩ := RunsEach.split_calls hr
      exact ⟨_, st₁, tr₂, by simp [List.append_assoc], .cons hd ha, hb⟩

/-- The frames an exit can pass: every frame but a running cleanup call's and a call's (`Passes`
for some exit). -/
def Frame.passable : Frame O → Bool
  | .resume _ | .ret .. => false
  | _ => true

/-- **A cleanup runs exactly the plan**: through frames an exit can pass, the calls of `plan pre`,
in order, each with its scope's locals, with each scope's drop between them, and nothing else. -/
theorem cleans_iff_runs_plan {P : Program O} :
    ∀ {pre : List (Frame O)} {L : AList Var O.Val} {st st' : O.Store} {tr : List O.Event},
    (∀ f ∈ pre, f.passable = true) →
    (Cleans P pre L st tr st' ↔ RunsEach P (planWith pre L) st tr st')
  | [], L, st, st', tr, _ => by
    constructor
    · intro h; cases h; exact .nil _
    · intro h; cases h; exact .nil _ _
  | .scope b dm ds :: pre, L, st, st', tr, hp => by
    have hp' := fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf)
    constructor
    · intro h
      cases h with
      | scope hr hc => exact RunsAll.each hr (.drop ((cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').1 hc))
    · intro h
      obtain ⟨tr₁, st₁, tr₂, rfl, ha, hb⟩ := RunsEach.split_calls h
      cases hb with
      | drop hb => exact .scope ha ((cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').2 hb)
  | .test l cr t b site dm :: pre, L, st, st', tr, hp => by
    have hp' := fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf)
    constructor
    · intro h; cases h with
      | test hc => exact .drop ((cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').1 hc)
    · intro h; cases h with
      | drop hb => exact .test ((cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').2 hb)
  | .seq _ :: pre, L, st, st', tr, hp => by
    have hp' := fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf)
    constructor
    · intro h; cases h with
      | seq hc => exact (cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').1 hc
    · intro h; exact .seq ((cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').2 h)
  | .body .. :: pre, L, st, st', tr, hp => by
    have hp' := fun f hf => hp f (List.mem_cons_of_mem _ hf)
    constructor
    · intro h; cases h with
      | body hc => exact (cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').1 hc
    · intro h; exact .body ((cleans_iff_runs_plan hp').2 h)
  | .resume _ :: _, _, _, _, _, hp => absurd (hp _ List.mem_cons_self) (by simp [Frame.passable])
  | .ret .. :: _, _, _, _, _, hp => absurd (hp _ List.mem_cons_self) (by simp [Frame.passable])

/-- Every frame an exit passes is passable. -/
theorem Passes.passable {e : Exit O.Val} : ∀ {f : Frame O}, Passes e f → f.passable = true
  | .seq _, _ | .scope .., _ | .test .., _ | .body .., _ => rfl
  | .resume _, h => h.elim
  | .ret .., h => h.elim

/-! ### Traps are terminal -/

theorem step_trapped (P : Program O) (kd : TrapKind) (site : SiteId) (L : AList Var O.Val)
    (st : O.Store) (k : List (Frame O)) : step P ⟨.trapped kd site, L, st, k⟩ = none := rfl

/-- After a trap nothing runs: no step, no event, and the frames are not unwound. -/
theorem steps_from_trapped {P : Program O} {kd : TrapKind} {site : SiteId} {L : AList Var O.Val}
    {st : O.Store} {k : List (Frame O)} {tr : List O.Event} {c : Config O}
    (h : Steps P ⟨.trapped kd site, L, st, k⟩ tr c) :
    tr = [] ∧ c = ⟨.trapped kd site, L, st, k⟩ := by
  cases h with
  | refl => exact ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | step hs _ =>
    cases hs with
    | silent h => simp [step_trapped] at h
    | prim h _ => simp [step_trapped] at h

/-- **`abort()` runs no cleanup.** From `abort()` the only step is to the trap, with the same
frames and state, and nothing follows: no deferred call runs and no event is emitted. -/
theorem abort_runs_no_cleanup {P : Program O} {site : SiteId} {L : AList Var O.Val}
    {st : O.Store} {k : List (Frame O)} {tr : List O.Event} {c : Config O}
    (h : Steps P ⟨.exec (.abort site), L, st, k⟩ tr c) :
    tr = [] ∧ (c = ⟨.exec (.abort site), L, st, k⟩ ∨ c = ⟨.trapped .abort site, L, st, k⟩) := by
  cases h with
  | refl => exact ⟨rfl, .inl rfl⟩
  | step hs hrest =>
    cases hs with
    | silent h =>
      simp only [step, Option.some.injEq, Out.next.injEq] at h
      subst h
      have := steps_from_trapped hrest
      exact ⟨by simpa [Label.events] using this.1, .inr this.2⟩
    | prim h _ => simp [step] at h

/-! ### Evaluation order and determinism -/

/-- The first argument that traps decides the trap, whatever follows it: once the arguments
before it evaluate, in whatever state they leave. -/
theorem evalArgs_first_trap (L : AList Var O.Val) (e : O.Expr) (kd : TrapKind)
    (es₂ : List O.Expr) :
    ∀ (es₁ : List O.Expr) (st st₁ : O.Store) (vs : List O.Val),
      evalArgs O L st es₁ = .ok (vs, st₁) → O.eval L st₁ e = .trap kd →
      evalArgs O L st (es₁ ++ e :: es₂) = .trap kd
  | [], st, st₁, _, h, he => by
    simp only [evalArgs, Res.ok.injEq, Prod.mk.injEq] at h
    obtain ⟨_, rfl⟩ := h
    simp [evalArgs, he, Res.bind]
  | e₁ :: es₁, st, st₁, vs, h, he => by
    show evalArgs O L st (e₁ :: (es₁ ++ e :: es₂)) = _
    cases h₁ : O.eval L st e₁ with
    | ok p =>
      obtain ⟨v₁, st₀⟩ := p
      cases h₂ : evalArgs O L st₀ es₁ with
      | ok q =>
        obtain ⟨vs₁, st₂⟩ := q
        have hst : st₂ = st₁ := by
          simp [evalArgs, h₁, h₂, Res.bind, Res.map] at h; exact h.2
        subst hst
        have := evalArgs_first_trap L e kd es₂ es₁ st₀ st₂ vs₁ h₂ he
        simp [evalArgs, h₁, this, Res.bind, Res.map]
      | trap _ => simp [evalArgs, h₁, h₂, Res.bind, Res.map] at h
      | stuck => simp [evalArgs, h₁, h₂, Res.bind, Res.map] at h
    | trap _ => simp [evalArgs, h₁, Res.bind] at h
    | stuck => simp [evalArgs, h₁, Res.bind] at h

/-- Steps of the machine itself are deterministic. -/
theorem step_silent_deterministic {P : Program O} {c c₁ c₂ : Config O}
    (h₁ : step P c = some (.next c₁)) (h₂ : step P c = some (.next c₂)) : c₁ = c₂ := by
  rw [h₁] at h₂; cases h₂; rfl

/-- **Determinism up to the environment.** Two steps from one configuration reach the same
configuration with the same label, unless the configuration requests a primitive operation: then
each step takes an answer the contract allows, is labeled by it and continues with it, so only the
environment's answers can differ, and equal answers give equal steps. -/
theorem step_deterministic {P : Program O} {c c₁ c₂ : Config O} {l₁ l₂ : Label O}
    (h₁ : Step P c l₁ c₁) (h₂ : Step P c l₂ c₂) :
    (l₁ = l₂ ∧ c₁ = c₂) ∨ ∃ p vs site st k a₁ a₂, step P c = some (.request p vs site st k) ∧
      O.allows p vs st a₁ = true ∧ O.allows p vs st a₂ = true ∧
      l₁ = a₁.label ∧ l₂ = a₂.label ∧ c₁ = k a₁ ∧ c₂ = k a₂ := by
  cases h₁ with
  | silent h =>
    cases h₂ with
    | silent h' => exact .inl ⟨rfl, step_silent_deterministic h h'⟩
    | prim h' _ => rw [h] at h'; cases h'
  | prim h ha =>
    cases h₂ with
    | silent h' => rw [h] at h'; cases h'
    | prim h' ha' =>
      rw [h] at h'; cases h'
      exact .inr ⟨_, _, _, _, _, _, _, h, ha, ha', rfl, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩

/-! ### Destruction -/

/-- The cleanup call that destroys a value calls the type's one `Destroy` function, which takes the
value as its only, ordinary parameter and returns `Unit`. -/
theorem destroyCall_sig {P : Program O} (D : DestroyProtocol P) (T : O.Ty) (arg : O.Expr)
    (site : SiteId) (d : Deferred O) (h : D.destroyCall T arg site = some d) :
    d.args = [arg] ∧ ∃ fn x, AList.lookup P.fns d.f = some fn ∧ fn.params = [(x, T)] ∧
      fn.result = O.unitTy := by
  unfold DestroyProtocol.destroyCall at h
  cases hi : D.impl T with
  | none => rw [hi] at h; cases h
  | some f =>
    rw [hi] at h; cases h
    exact ⟨rfl, D.sig T f hi⟩

/-- A type with a `Destroy` function is not `Copy`. -/
theorem destroy_not_copy {P : Program O} (D : DestroyProtocol P) (T : O.Ty) (f : FnId)
    (h : D.impl T = some f) : O.isCopy T = false := D.notCopy T f h

/-- A sequence's element `i` is visited at position `i`: in increasing index order. -/
theorem seqDestroyOrder_elem (n i : Nat) (h : i < n) : (seqDestroyOrder n)[i]? = some (.elem i) := by
  rw [seqDestroyOrder, List.getElem?_append_left (by simpa using h)]
  simp [h]

/-- A sequence's backing is released last, after all `n` elements. -/
theorem seqDestroyOrder_storage (n : Nat) :
    (seqDestroyOrder n).length = n + 1 ∧ (seqDestroyOrder n)[n]? = some .storage := by
  simp [seqDestroyOrder]

/-- The entries of a map, in visiting order. -/
private def entryParts (n : Nat) : List Part := (List.range n).flatMap fun i => [.key i, .value i]

private theorem entryParts_succ (n : Nat) :
    entryParts (n + 1) = entryParts n ++ [.key n, .value n] := by
  simp [entryParts, List.range_succ, List.flatMap_append]

private theorem entryParts_length : ∀ n, (entryParts n).length = 2 * n
  | 0 => rfl
  | n + 1 => by rw [entryParts_succ]; simp [entryParts_length n]; omega

private theorem entryParts_get : ∀ (n i : Nat), i < n →
    (entryParts n)[2 * i]? = some (.key i) ∧ (entryParts n)[2 * i + 1]? = some (.value i)
  | 0, _, h => absurd h (Nat.not_lt_zero _)
  | n + 1, i, h => by
    rw [entryParts_succ]
    by_cases hi : i < n
    · have hl := entryParts_length n
      rw [List.getElem?_append_left (by omega), List.getElem?_append_left (by omega)]
      exact entryParts_get n i hi
    · have hin : i = n := by omega
      subst hin
      have hl := entryParts_length i
      rw [List.getElem?_append_right (by omega), List.getElem?_append_right (by omega), hl]
      simp

/-- A map's `i`-th entry (in ascending key order) is visited key first, at position `2i`, then
value, at `2i+1`. -/
theorem mapDestroyOrder_entry (n i : Nat) (h : i < n) :
    (mapDestroyOrder n)[2 * i]? = some (.key i) ∧
      (mapDestroyOrder n)[2 * i + 1]? = some (.value i) := by
  have hl := entryParts_length n
  have := entryParts_get n i h
  show (entryParts n ++ [Part.storage])[2 * i]? = _ ∧
    (entryParts n ++ [Part.storage])[2 * i + 1]? = _
  rw [List.getElem?_append_left (by omega), List.getElem?_append_left (by omega)]
  exact this

/-- A map's nodes are released last, after all `n` entries. -/
theorem mapDestroyOrder_storage (n : Nat) :
    (mapDestroyOrder n).length = 2 * n + 1 ∧ (mapDestroyOrder n)[2 * n]? = some .storage := by
  have hl := entryParts_length n
  show (entryParts n ++ [Part.storage]).length = _ ∧ (entryParts n ++ [Part.storage])[2 * n]? = _
  rw [List.getElem?_append_right (by omega), hl]
  simp [hl]

/-! ### Non-vacuity

The cleanup laws assume that each deferred call completes (`Completes`). Real calls meet it: in the
demo language of `Spec/Exec.lean`, a deferred `log(1)` completes from any locals, in any state and
on any frames, emitting `1` (its parameter is a fresh local of a new activation); a deferred
`bump()` completes silently and adds one to the counter. -/

theorem demo_log_completes (L : AList Var Demo.ops.Val) (st : Demo.ops.Store) :
    Completes Demo.prog L ⟨Demo.logF, [.lit 1], Demo.s⟩ st [1] st := by
  intro k
  refine steps_next rfl (steps_request (O := Demo.ops) (P := Demo.prog)
    (a := ⟨some (1 : Int), .ok (0 : Int), Demo.DState.life (.enter [Demo.x]) st⟩) (tr := []) rfl
    (by simp [Demo.ops, Demo.logFn]) ?_)
  exact steps_next rfl (steps_next rfl (Steps.refl _))

theorem demo_bump_completes (L : AList Var Demo.ops.Val) (st : Demo.ops.Store) :
    Completes Demo.prog L ⟨Demo.bumpF, [], Demo.s⟩ st [] { st with counter := st.counter + 1 } := by
  intro k
  refine steps_next rfl (steps_request (O := Demo.ops) (P := Demo.prog)
    (a := ⟨none, .ok (0 : Int),
      { Demo.DState.life (.enter []) st with counter := st.counter + 1 }⟩) (tr := []) rfl
    (by simp [Demo.ops, Demo.DState.life]) ?_)
  exact steps_next rfl (steps_next rfl (Steps.refl _))

/-- So the cleanup law applies to a real scope: the end of a block that registered `log(1)` and
then `bump()` runs `bump` (silently, adding one) and then `log`, from any state. -/
example (L : AList Var Demo.ops.Val) (st : Demo.DState) (k : List (Frame Demo.ops))
    (dm : List Var) :
    Steps Demo.prog ⟨.done, L, st, .scope ⟨1⟩ dm [⟨Demo.bumpF, [], Demo.s⟩,
      ⟨Demo.logF, [.lit 1], Demo.s⟩] :: k⟩ [1]
      ⟨.done, restrict dm L, dropOut Demo.ops dm L { st with counter := st.counter + 1 }, k⟩ :=
  done_scope_runs_defers Demo.prog ⟨1⟩ dm L k
    (.cons (demo_bump_completes L st) (.cons (demo_log_completes L _) (.nil _)))

end ConcreteSpec.Core.Exec
# C5: protected C1 lifecycle interface blocks part 1

Inspected base: `80b23d59dd3a95affbae2f04ccb220c072801cdc`, branch `wip/spec-c5`.
No specification source was changed. No decision was made.

## QUESTIONS

The brief requires the lifecycle operations (`bind`, `enter`, `leave`, `drop`) to be
partial, with `none` meaning refused/stuck, and requires an `Operands.life`-compatible
function. C1 currently exposes only:

```lean
life : Life → Store → Store
```

There is no lifecycle refusal channel. This matters independently of how C5
represents places, loans, or invariants.

A concrete execution path is the last scope exit. In `Spec/Exec.lean`, `step`
on `⟨.done, L, st, [.scope b dm []]⟩` unconditionally produces

```lean
some (.next ⟨.done, restrict dm L, dropOut O dm L st, []⟩)
```

where `dropOut` unconditionally calls `O.life (.drop (dead dm L)) st`.
`Config.final?` classifies that resulting configuration as `some .normal`,
without consulting its store. Thus `run` reports normal completion even if the
lifecycle adapter records a refusal in its store. This path has no intervening
operand evaluation or primitive contract check that could detect that refusal.
The call-return paths likewise unconditionally apply `life .leave` and resume
the caller; binding/assignment unconditionally applies `life (.bind x)`.

For example, when dropping a local root is refused because a still-live loan
would outlive it, the required partial operation can return `none`, but this
machine transition cannot be made stuck through `life` alone. Returning the
old state silently retains an out-of-scope root; deleting the loan repairs the
refused state; recording a poison bit or `Option.none` still lets the final
scope exit complete normally. None implements the requested refusal contract.
A checker premise excluding all refused lifecycle events would be a weaker,
conditional integration contract and would need to be explicitly agreed.

Recommended interface repair: let `life` return `Option Store` and propagate
failure to stuckness at every lifecycle call site, including the initial
`enter` path and primitive-result binding. The latter currently constructs a
configuration through a total request continuation, so changing the field's
type alone is insufficient. This requires changes to protected `Exec.lean`
and its proofs/examples.

Alternative: add an explicit instrumentation-validity check to C1, checked
before steps and before finality, and specify how a refused lifecycle event
marks the store invalid. This also changes protected C1 and its laws.

Which integration contract should C1 provide? I recommend explicit partial
lifecycle transitions because they express the brief's `none = refused/stuck`
rule directly, without a second failure protocol.

## Disposition

The brief says: “if C5 needs a change there, stop and list it under QUESTIONS.”
Stopped at this interface boundary. Part 1 and therefore part 2 remain
unimplemented; no existing proof was changed. There are no new theorems or
commits. No gates were run because no build input changed. This report is a
scratch artifact in the existing ignored `_review/` directory.
# Concrete Lean foundation proposal — draft 3

Status: proposal, not a completed freeze or approval of new rules. On 2026-11-07 the owner said, verbatim, **"ok to option b then for freeze the foundation>?"** (owner answer supplied with this revision). This approves writing the shared foundation before freezing it; **pending recording in docs/decisions.md**. It does not approve the new questions in section 5.

The [A2i decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06) freezes integrated scopes, "not an untested whole-language architecture". Here the frozen foundation means interfaces and laws implemented, proved and tested together, with representative interactions. It does not mean designing every future slice before slice 1. Later slices supply lawful instances; changes to decided rules return to the owner.

References below use the actual anchors in this checkout. **Core rows** means [Q1–Q9](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core). The pipeline referred to in the brief as the 2026-11-07 decision is recorded here under [the 2026-11-06 pipeline anchor](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06); there is no `decided-2026-11-07-pipeline` anchor. The [authoritative core-row notes](../docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) supplement these rows. Proposed additions without a decided row are identified as such.

## 1. The freeze boundary

Freeze **extensible interfaces and their laws**, not an exhaustive list of future types, grants, instructions or events. Every admitted instance must provide the required laws; an unknown instance is refused, never silently given semantics. Extensions must preserve the old fragment's judgments and executions. A new operation cannot change what an old operation means.

The foundation comprises target profiles; typed identities; types, values and places; control and calls; scalar operations; layout and representation; byte memory; ownership, loans, brands and authority; observations and leakage; evaluator adequacy; primitive contracts; frames and ABI; evidence, facts and diagnostics. An interface includes its state, preconditions, results and preservation laws, not just a record of names.

Before freezing, implement these contracts in the authoritative Lean library, with readable comments, accepted/refused examples and counterexamples to invalid premises. Discharge the four Q9.14 design theorems and the [A2i interaction/erasure obligations](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06), including the laws below. Tests alone do not establish them. Existing `ConcreteSpec` fragments are starting material, not evidence that omitted pointer fragments, external frames, initialization or cleanup interactions are already covered. Review and owner approval precede the freeze. This is a writing proposal, not that completed review or approval.

## 2. Contracts to finish before the freeze

These are contracts to complete, not declarations that the proofs exist. Decided rules remain binding. New semantic completions and revisions are separately answerable in section 5; none is approved by this draft.

### C1. Execution, calls and cleanup

**Freeze:** machine configurations with current command, locals, places, call frames and structured continuations; a labeled single-step relation; results for ordinary completion, return, labeled break/continue and terminal abort. A continuation says where execution resumes. Prove sequencing, scope restoration and cleanup preservation, including effects while evaluating an exit value.

**Rule:** evaluate subexpressions completely left to right: receiver before arguments, left operand before right, literal fields/elements in written order, base before index. Assignment evaluates its RHS before any destination subexpression. `&&` evaluates its RHS only if the LHS is true; `||` only if false; both operands are still checked. Resolve calls by typed identity; evaluate arguments before entering the callee. Moves transfer ownership exactly once; copies require a declared, checked Copy instance.

Keep the decided Core loop condition region, counted-loop bound snapshots and counter advancement. Preserve the restrictions on exits inside operands. Return evaluates its result before leaving scopes. Normal block exit, return, break and continue run the deferred calls of each exited scope, inner scope first and reverse registration order within a scope. Deferred operands are read at cleanup, with dependencies kept alive. If cleanup aborts, stop immediately; no remaining cleanup runs. Explicit abort runs no deferred call and does not unwind. Ordinary locals do not acquire implicit user destructors: explicit consumption, storage teardown and resource destruction remain different operations. Q2.3 fixes sequence destruction in increasing index order, then backing; OrderedMap in ascending key order, key before value, then nodes; Heap payload before cell; Option/Result destroy the present payload. User types follow their checked destroy bodies. Q3.8 explicitly preserves index order for zero-sized elements. Destruction is an ordinary checked call and may itself fail to terminate unless stronger evidence exists.

**Authority:** Core Q2.1–Q2.7, Q6.5, Q9.1, Q9.3–Q9.4, Q9.8, Q9.11; [OD10](../docs/decisions.md#od10), [OD53](../docs/decisions.md#od53), [OD69](../docs/decisions.md#od69); STMT-4/5. **Later:** source constructs and their lowering, loop/call bounds and destructor bodies at their slices. No postfix `?`, exceptions or implicit error propagation is introduced.

### C2. Scalars, failure and arithmetic

**Freeze:** mathematical scalar values, finite bit representations, operation contracts and error classification; representation and operation correspondence laws. Integer widths remain those named by `i8`…`i64` and `u8`…`u64`; proposed `usize` is separate. A bool has only false/true, encoded as byte 0/1. TYPE-20 makes char a one-byte code unit: every u8 round-trips through char. OD137 restricts character literals to ASCII; it does not restrict stored char values to ASCII.

**Rule:** signed representation is two's complement. Division truncates toward zero; nonzero remainder has the dividend's sign. Zero divisors and signed `MIN / -1` and `MIN % -1` are errors, the latter two `Overflow`. Shift counts satisfy `0 ≤ count < width`; valid shifts discard outgoing bits, signed right shift sign-fills and unsigned right shift zero-fills. Plain arithmetic needs closed safety evidence. Checked operations return the decided `Result<T, ArithmeticError>`; wrapping operations compute modulo the width and saturating operations clamp to bounds. `as` is lossless over the entire source domain; lossy conversions use named operations. OD106 and ARITH-10 fix `try_convert -> Result<T, OutOfRange>`, separate from ArithmeticError.

Keep Q6.7's separation of traps, unsafe stuckness, allocation failure and evaluator fuel exhaustion. Handled checked errors are values, not traps; explicit abort is terminal. Safe admission requires evidence excluding implicit traps. The exact trap taxonomy is question 9; ArithmeticError does not determine it. UTF-8 operation failures belong to TYPE-3 and Q4.6–Q4.7, with the remaining classification in question 11.


**Authority:** [no hidden traps](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-no-hidden-traps), [OD11](../docs/decisions.md#od11), [OD99](../docs/decisions.md#od99), [OD106](../docs/decisions.md#od106), [OD114](../docs/decisions.md#od114), TYPE-19, Core Q6.7. **Later:** operation syntax and admission instances; float rules at slice 26. The interface admits float instances but does not invent their unsettled details. No `isize` is added by implication: pointer differences use a mathematical signed offset in the model and an explicitly contracted representable result in source APIs.

### C3. Target, layout and representation

**Freeze:** a well-formed target profile and a layout interface. Separate address width, pointer storage size/alignment, length width, byte order and address spaces; do not infer any from CPU arithmetic width. The profile specifies pointer encoding, distinguished null, accessible spaces and maximum object sizes. Require representable sizes/offsets and checked layout arithmetic; reject an unrepresentable layout rather than wrap. No universal 64-bit assumption, including for 20/24-bit addresses.

Layout laws: positive power-of-two alignment; size a multiple of aggregate alignment; occupied field bytes in bounds and non-overlapping; required field alignment in native/C modes; unique discriminants; and valid tag decoding. Only if question 5 adopts niches (unused encodings used for variants), require sound niche decoding and eligibility proofs. No niche machinery is required while niches are absent. Zero-size fields have empty byte ranges and may share offsets. Packed mode replaces natural field alignment with explicit unaligned-access premises; it must not falsely prove packed fields naturally aligned. Core uses the hybrid state in C4, with target/layout as a read-only parameter for sizeof/alignof and raw access. Safe typed operations are proved representation-independent where their premises permit it. The concrete algorithm below is one implementation proving these laws, not the premise of every earlier proof.

**Rule:** native structs retain declaration order, each field rounded up to its alignment, aggregate alignment the maximum (1 for empty), and final size rounded up. Empty aggregates have size 0/alignment 1. Arrays have size `n × size(T)` and alignment `align(T)`, including `n = 0`. Fixed-width integer storage respects value width; alignment is target-defined. Q4.5 decides only StaticStr’s explicit pointer/byte-length FFI adapter, without an internal ABI promise. Q3.1–Q3.4 and Q4.6–Q4.7 decide view roots, loans and ranges, not their physical representation. Text length counts UTF-8 bytes. TYPE-3 Text owns immutable bytes, never aliases another value’s buffer, and is linear. Its ownership, allocator dependency and sufficient release context must survive representation; Q4.5’s FFI adapter does not fix Text’s internal size. No universal two-word Text layout is frozen. A common shared/mutable view representation is a proposal, not a decided rule. Defer its concrete layout to the view/layout slice, preserving the decided second-class semantics; no two-word view ABI is frozen. Address-space selection is explicit profile metadata, including program-memory text: no silent cast to data pointers. `Secret<T>` has T's layout if question 18 approves it; layout never implies constant time.

Native enum layout and niche policy are separate questions 4 and 5. Until answered, retain the existing profile rules; OD72 still refuses empty-enum source declarations. Invalid raw tags do not become valid typed values.

Include native, C and packed modes now. C layout/calling details come from the selected ABI; enums remain forbidden across extern boundaries. Packed structs use declaration order/no padding/alignment 1; C+packed uses C order with packing overriding alignment. Misaligned fields are permitted but references to them are refused. Direct field access uses an explicitly justified unaligned operation, possibly lowered to byte accesses. Preserve FFI-4's existing `align(N)` restriction; surface repr forms wait for slice 25. Stronger over-alignment, MMIO and section-placement syntax remain deferred to workloads. No shortened MCU trap format is adopted.

**Authority:** [runtime/layout decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06), Core Q3.5–Q3.11, Q4.1–Q4.5; [OD72](../docs/decisions.md#od72), [OD110](../docs/decisions.md#od110), [OD111](../docs/decisions.md#od111), [OD126](../docs/decisions.md#od126), [OD147](../docs/decisions.md#od147); FFI-4/7/8/10. Revisions are listed below. **Later:** syntax, additional target/ABI implementations, concrete allocation and FFI operations; not the layout laws or alignment premises.

### C4. Byte memory and value validity

**Freeze:** blocks with fresh allocation identity, address space, base, extent, alignment and lifetime; byte initialization state; pointer provenance (the allocation a pointer originates from); a typed representation relation `Rep`; partial decoding, access validity and disjoint-memory composition laws. Pointer fragments carry provenance metadata separately from numeric bytes. Do not introduce a poison value that can become an arbitrary typed result.

**State choice (question 2):** Core and its Lean evaluator use one hybrid state: structured places for safe typed storage, plus byte/provenance blocks for raw and exposed storage. A partial place-to-block/range projection and Rep relate the two. They are not independently writable copies. Safe borrow-view formation retains the structured root/projection and does not itself materialize bytes. Raw address-taking, expose, or passing an address to foreign code materializes the containing storage with a stable block/range mapping; existing materialized storage is reused. sizeof/alignof query layout without materializing storage. Materialization encodes active fields and pointer fragments and selects allowed initialization/byte contents for padding once through C8’s oracle.

On a typed write to materialized storage, encode the new active value and update its bytes, initialization and provenance; preserve every byte, initialization bit and fragment outside the new active representation, including padding. Do not query fresh padding choices on this write. Newly active bytes must be initialized by the write; newly inactive bytes retain their previous contents. Lowerings must prove this store rule, including enum variant changes. An explicit raw copy follows its own byte-copy contract.

Raw writes update bytes/fragments and invalidate overlapping cached typed values. Every safe typed read of a materialized place itself applies partial Rep decoding to the current bytes, initialization, provenance and permissions; no user-level decode instruction is needed. Successful decoding supplies the valid value. Failure is unsafe stuckness, never a fabricated value or implicit repair; safe-admission preservation must exclude it under the stated trusted premises. Unmaterialized typed reads use structured values. Raw pointers, expose/from_exposed and sizeof/alignof therefore already have Core meanings. Layout is a read-only parameter. Memory CFG makes this representation and ABI explicit for every operation; it does not introduce raw memory for the first time. This keeps typed proofs abstract while allowing slice 24 to execute without replacing Core state.

**Rule:** memory bytes are initialized octets or uninitialized. Allocation starts uninitialized unless its primitive contract initializes it. Track initialization per occupied field/element; moving out removes that slot's typed ownership/read permission. Reading a typed value requires its active representation bytes initialized and valid. Padding and inactive enum storage need not be initialized and cannot affect typed equality, typed control or typed observations. Initialized padding may be observed through authorized raw byte reads and then affect output. Representation simulations preserve such observed bytes, or explicitly relate the same allowed padding choices; they cannot erase a raw observation as irrelevant nondeterminism. Raw byte copying may transport uninitialized padding and provenance fragments without observing them; observing a byte requires initialization. `Rep` ignores padding but checks active tags, bool/char/text validity and pointer fragments. Prove encoding/decoding round-trip for valid typed values, with allowed padding choices, and that decoding yields only valid values.

Block identity is never reused, even if an address is. Deallocation ends all access rights to the old block. Nonzero accesses need provenance, bounds, liveness, alignment (or justified unaligned mode), initialization for reads and write permission for writes. Q3.7’s zero-byte raw access needs only alignment and a non-null address. One-past pointers may be formed but not dereferenced. Address arithmetic cannot silently wrap into another allocation. Integers carry no provenance; `expose` records the block in the address space, and `from_exposed` uses Q3.9's in-bounds-first, then one-past, then no-provenance rule over live, nonempty eligible exposed blocks. Do not add a new arbitrary tie-break where the decided relation permits choices. Pointer equality follows the decided raw model, not equality of allocation identities alone.

Zero-size slots own distinct logical obligations, are destroyed in index order (Q3.8), occupy no bytes and may share addresses; address equality does not merge owners. Typed operations still discharge their logical ownership obligations; this does not strengthen the zero-byte raw-access rule. Address collisions mean overlapping occupied bytes, not merely equal bases. Address exhaustion belongs to allocator availability. Allocation failure is a typed result conserving the rejected input, not an implicit trap. Require Q1.11's allocator availability laws: progress under availability, unchanged allocator state on failure, release never reducing availability, and a concrete success witness. Callback effects are not rolled back; every element remains accounted for in its typed result. Raw/foreign writes must re-establish validity before safe typed use; violating a trusted premise yields no safety claim, not a fabricated valid value.

**Authority:** Core Q1.8–Q1.12, Q3.7–Q3.11; [memory decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06). Hybrid state, initialization/padding and pointer-fragment completions require question 2; they do not revise Q3.7’s exception. **Later:** raw-pointer syntax (24), allocator algorithms (19–23), FFI operations (25). They instantiate this model rather than replace it.

### C5. Dynamic borrow, brands and authority

**Freeze:** a preservation invariant relating owner slots, moves, root/projection identities, active loans and parent loans, generative brands, dependency lifetimes, grant/resource identities and operation rights. Prove preservation on each step and across calls, including ghost erasure.

**Rule:** loans retain the original root and projection. Keep **whole-root** conflicts, including disjoint fields: an exclusive loan conflicts with every other active access to that root; shared loans freeze the whole owner against mutation/move. A reborrow suspends conflicting access through its parent until the child ends. A loan-derived pointer is valid only during the loan. Shared views are Copy; mutable views are nonduplicable and end by borrow discipline, without an exactly-once destruction obligation. No optimization exploits exclusivity before the checker guarantee is proved.

Fresh brand binders are nominal, including reset/loop generations; dependencies cannot escape their lifetime. Child allocators own backing and expose parent dependence. Grant narrowing consumes and intersects rights; acquisition preserves the parent as decided; close/give-up consumes its resource on all specified outcomes. Zero representation size never removes linear obligations. Permissions in `with(...)` are erased sets, distinct from runtime grants. `GrantTy` describes launch-grant types; File/Socket/Listener are acquired handles. Clock kind has separate identity/resolution/guarantee metadata. Entropy’s source is a named premise under the randomness decision; its exact grant shape and ending contract wait for slice 6 (question 14).

Heap handles contain a pointer and sufficient stable release context, with any erasure requiring question 6 and a proof that release is statically known and needs no per-instance information. Allocator/grant representations are opaque per resource; grant state erases only if stream/device identity, rights and lifecycle are statically determined and all authority transitions are preserved. Prove erasure, rather than assuming one allocator or one capability means zero state.

**Authority:** Core Q1.1–Q1.7, Q3.1–Q3.4, Q7.1–Q7.11; [OD15](../docs/decisions.md#od15), [OD23](../docs/decisions.md#od23), [authority decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority), [randomness](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness). **Later:** concrete loan/grant operations and source rules; no choice of a new stacked/tree borrow model replacing whole-root rules.

### C6. Observations, environment and concurrency extension

**Freeze:** extensible typed event signatures, request/response matching, finite and infinite traces, terminal observations and an environment relation. A trace is the ordered externally relevant history. Prove preservation of full observations, allowing internal steps that emit no event, without turning infinite internal execution into successful termination.

**Rule:** model startup, capability requests and responses (including blocking), external/FFI effects, public output, logical terminal actions and shutdown. Bind external choices to a shared input/environment history. Error, partial-delivery and blocking behavior belongs to each operation contract; do not assume a hostile environment cooperates. Preserve output preceding a trap and externally visible final state. Normal exit and trap exit 134 remain different observations. Missing launch grants refuse startup with 125. Traps retain kind, typed site and the exact decided line `concrete: trap: <kind> at <path>:<line>:<col> (site <id>)`, project-relative forward-slash path, 1-based line and column; no per-build abbreviated variant. The kind inventory/rendering and byte-column/tab convention are questions 9–10. No abbreviated MCU format is introduced.

An environment response may be nondeterministic but must satisfy its contract. Distinguish modeled failure, unmodeled foreign behavior and broken premises. Exhaustion of an allocator can return its specified error; stack guarantees remain subject to the exact artifact's checked bound, reservation and launcher premises. Do not invent a universal resource-exhaustion trap. Fuel exhaustion is an evaluator report, never an external event.

Concurrency is deferred to slice 28. Sequential theorems assume a single thread and no interference. Any future extension must preserve complete executions of that fragment under those premises; deleting synchronization events from interfering executions is insufficient. No concurrent memory model is chosen here.

**Authority:** Core Q6.7, Q7.5–Q7.10, Q8.4–Q8.6; [runtime](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06), [stack bounds](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness), [concurrency last](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-final-slices). The sequential preservation obligation is an interface law, not a chosen concurrent model. **Later:** grant-specific events and adapters; concurrency and atomic laws at slice 28, last. Public environment effects are not deterministic merely because input replay is deterministic.

### C7. Secret leakage and accounting

**Freeze:** a separate leakage trace and public-observation relation; public/secret state equivalence; explicit release-site records; byte/resource accounting and their preservation/erasure laws. Leakage means information visible to the baseline timing observer, not ordinary I/O.

**Rule:** leakage records branch decisions, exact addresses, allocation sizes, loop counts and operands of variable-time operations, with public lengths and contracted external effects. A release record carries ordered (typed site, released value), with reason/path metadata for facts. The release-policy interface lists allowed functions/sites or forbids release, checks reachable releases through calls, and permits only explicit declared values. Its security obligation is delimited release: for equal public inputs, matched external choices and equal ordered release site/value traces, prove equal public observations (NI), and separately equal leakage traces (CT). Policy authorization alone does not establish either theorem or allow other secret effects. These are distinct decided claims, not an optional variable-time Secret tier.

Preserve wipe events as effects optimization cannot erase. Per allocator lineage report requested bytes, net change and peak logical bytes, plus allocator-defined footprint, each a sound bound with status or “not established”. Release/reset conserve accounting, and zero-size ownership adds no invented bytes. Bounds use polynomials with max over counts, lengths and depths; stack remains separate. This is the A2i instrumentation of R-0592, not later slice analysis.


**Authority:** [information flow](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06), [memory/accounting](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06), Core Q9.10. **Later:** scalar secret rules/proofs at 7, arrays/wipes at 15 and heaps at 19. Binary constant time, hardware timing and actual wiping remain named backend premises until proved; speculative leakage is outside the decided observer model.

### C8. Evaluator adequacy and termination

**Freeze:** both the small-step relation and Q9.9's total fuel-indexed evaluator over instrumented hybrid Core state: `eval(target, layout, environmentChoices, fuel, coreProgram, state)`. This is the reference evaluator for qualification; it executes Core, not BIR or source text. Choices explicitly cover external responses, allocation/address choices, exposed-pointer alternatives and padding materialization. A choice input can be an oracle rather than a finite stream; each answer is checked against the operation contract. Oracle queries are pure deterministic functions of the Core execution history, request kind, logical site and occurrence number, independent of fuel budget; answers are deterministic functions of those queries and the fixed oracle. Materialization queries occur only at C4’s first raw exposure, not on safe borrowing or typed rewrites. Fixed inputs make evaluation deterministic. Native backends need not issue the same queries; P4 checks allowed Core observations instead. Prove evaluator soundness (a reported result has a matching finite execution), completeness (every finite execution is obtained with enough fuel), monotonicity (extra fuel preserves a completed result) and trace-prefix consistency. For nondeterministic effects these statements quantify over the same environment choices.

Prove infinite-behavior adequacy too: compatible limits of fuel-indexed execution prefixes correspond to infinite maximal executions, and every such execution has those prefixes. Carry states and steps as well as event traces: silent divergence has infinitely many steps but only finitely many events; infinite interaction has infinitely many events. An endlessly blocked run retains its blocking classification. Independent finite witnesses do not establish a compatible infinite run. This follows Q9.9 and [A1, “Fuel and divergence”](../legacy/docs/compiler/A1_BOUNDARIES.md).

**Rule:** `outOfFuel` means only insufficient simulation budget. It proves neither divergence nor program failure. Divergence is an infinite execution; a blocked request is separately represented. Termination evidence is a theorem about all allowed runs under its named premises, not the evaluator returning once. Carry per-function termination permissions and evidence dependencies. Resource bounds count specified abstract operations; they are neither elapsed time nor evaluator implementation fuel.

**Authority:** Core Q6.6–Q6.7, Q9.9, [bounded execution](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-no-hidden-traps), [step bounds](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-step-bounds). **Later:** concrete variants, loop/call cost instances and user proofs. Q9.9 is retained, not replaced by small-step semantics.

### C9. Identities, primitive contracts, evidence and ghost erasure

**Freeze:** distinct typed identities for declarations/types, functions, binders/places, fields/variants, instances, sites, loans, brands, blocks, grants and obligations; capture-avoiding substitution and renaming laws. Names are presentation only. One primitive-contract interface supplies parameter/result types, authority, ownership/dependencies, pre/postconditions, memory footprint, initialization/validity, failures, events/leakage, resource effects and termination obligations. Every registered primitive supplies a complete contract; there is no catch-all unknown primitive.

**Rule:** use independent typing, ownership, authority and execution judgments, with checker equivalence and elaborator correspondence. Keep safety evidence bound to typed identities, target, contracts and checker/spec versions. Recheck generic substitutions and Mono admission; compose checked summaries across calls/modules without reading bodies to guess effects. Struct-invariant rules remain those in the SPARK decision: private-field struct invariants hold at every exposure, with the spec definition written before freeze; the source feature waits for slice 17 (question 15). Contracts are erased logical assertions. Q9.11 and OD144 put ensures after all deferred cleanup, observing the result and only surviving parameter state. A consumed or scope-cleaned parameter is unavailable; body locals are never in function-contract scope. Entry values are read by requires, not implicitly saved for ensures. Q9.11’s authoritative row notes, [OD117](../docs/decisions.md#od117) and [CON-3](../legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md#CON-3) explicitly forbid `old` and snapshot constructs. Express relations to pre-state values of consumed parameters as Lean theorems over entry and normal-return states, without retaining a runtime owner or reviving cleaned storage. Safety admission needs closed evidence; assumptions cannot manufacture it.

Prove erasure of ownership/brand/loan and accounting instrumentation and ghost computation: projected concrete executions and observations agree, and ghost state cannot alter runtime branches, authority or resources. Preserve diagnostic/evidence links even where code erases. Facts and diagnostics use one typed evidence graph and one versioned schema, with stable source sites; machine JSON views derive from it. A schema extension does not reinterpret old records.

**Authority:** Core Q1.5, Q6.3, Q9.1–Q9.2, Q9.6–Q9.7, Q9.10–Q9.13; [OD81](../docs/decisions.md#od81), [OD125](../docs/decisions.md#od125), [pipeline](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [early evidence](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence). **Later:** primitive instances and syntax, module/invariant checking at 17. Q9.7's exhaustive inventory becomes exhaustive coverage of the selected registry, not a permanently closed universe.

### C10. Frames and ABI

**Freeze:** logical memory footprints and the frame law (an operation preserves disjoint memory outside its permitted footprint); call-frame ownership/restoration; ABI signatures relating typed arguments/results to registers, stack slots and preserved machine state. Include frame size/alignment, return convention, clobbers, stack-accounting handoff, extern validity/retention and launcher entry/exit relations.

**Rule:** internal calls use an explicit target convention proved compatible with these contracts. At Memory CFG, lower the hybrid Core representation to explicit byte/provenance operations and ABI calls, proving its simulation against C4’s projection. BIR carries the complete resulting contract. Frame accounting binds to the final artifact, including spills, cleanup and launcher/runtime contributions, not merely source locals. Stack facts are separate from heap accounting. Foreign retention needs the decided `kept`/`Kept<H>` contract; FFI-safe types and no-enum restriction remain. Logical frame theorems do not add a source frame clause or snapshot construct.

**Authority:** Core Q3.12, Q8.5–Q8.6, [pipeline](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [contracts/P18](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-contracts), [stack bounds](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness). **Later:** ABI implementations, extern syntax at 25, stronger program frame proofs. Neither the frame interface nor packed access waits until then.

## 3. Pipeline and validation contract

Retain the decided stages:

`bytes → lexer → LL(1) parser → AST → resolve → HIR → elaboration → Core → checker → CheckedCore → Mono Core → Place MIR → Memory CFG → SSA → BIR`.

BIR feeds the LLVM printer or the proved BIR→LCB adapter. **Memory CFG stays the single layout/ABI/provenance lowering boundary**; retract P2's folding proposal. Earlier semantics uses C4’s hybrid state and read-only layout interface; representation simulations relate it to this explicit lowering boundary.

**Frontend:** retract P1's parse/print criterion. Define byte/token/grammar acceptance independently of the lexer/parser implementation, and source binding, typing and source-to-Core correspondence independently of resolve/elaboration. Prove lexer/parser correspondence to the independent grammar, resolution's binding preservation and elaboration's preservation of meaning, sequencing, cleanup, identities and source sites. Prove checker equivalence to independent admission judgments. Parse/print tests can supplement these proofs but cannot replace them. This retains Core Q8.3/Q9.13 and the decided direct frontend proofs.

Keep direct proofs for sequencing, cleanup, representation and the BIR→LCB adapter. Preserve the decided option of **proved validators for Mono and SSA**, as well as SSA optimizations, register allocation and linking. A validator's acceptance theorem establishes the required correspondence; running a checker without that theorem is not proof. Mono re-admits its output.

**Certificates and maximal behavior:** retain the [compiler-execution decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): every authoritative compilation emits kernel-checked certificates binding the exact source bytes through independent judgments/validated edges to the exact emitted BIR, and binding the actual backend input to it. A digest establishes identity, not semantic correspondence. Native execution of proved Lean definitions is not itself proof; any gap retains explicit compiler/runtime/toolchain assumptions. After slice 0 use the decided per-edge certificates.

Each lowering proves refinement of maximal behaviors, including termination, abort/trap, blocking and infinite execution with their traces under matched allowed choices. Target steps may stutter finitely; a progress or well-founded argument excludes infinite silent target stuttering that replaces source progress or completion. Also relate each finite target prefix to compatible source prefixes; their limits must satisfy C8’s infinite-behavior adequacy, not use unrelated witnesses. The default method is forward simulation with finite stuttering and target determinism under fixed admissible choices, yielding backward refinement once receptiveness, progress, initial/final correspondence and the required determinacy hypotheses are proved. Where choices cannot be fixed compatibly or these hypotheses fail, prove backward simulation directly (or an equivalent behavior-inclusion theorem). Do not assume all source programs terminate to justify stuttering. See [A1, “Fuel and divergence” and “Behaviour refinement”](../legacy/docs/compiler/A1_BOUNDARIES.md).

**Native closure (P3):** retain Q8.1’s proved source→BIR, qualified native and conditional image claim, LP0 after slice 1 and R-0505’s later final-image closure. Question 8 separately asks whether to advance it to 0.1. Any validator must bind exact linked image bytes, encodings, relocations, symbols, entry state, launcher/ABI, retained dependencies and target to LCB instruction semantics. Hardware/ISA correspondence, loader/OS/environment, foreign/runtime code, adequate stack and timing/wipe premises remain named unless discharged. LLVM stays unverified.

**Qualification (P4):** run LLVM, LCB and the hybrid Core Lean evaluator on **every supported example and controlled test**, with the same input bytes, target/layout profile, grant configuration and compatible scripted external responses. Frontend certificates bind this Core program to the source; representation simulations relate its raw-byte/layout observations to both native paths. For each native run, check that its complete trace is an allowed observation of a Core execution under those inputs: this is a refinement check. The decided backend-order rule stays: LLVM and LCB full observations must equal each other and the expected outcome on every supported example, and any mismatch fails (question 17). Supported examples therefore do not observe allocation-dependent values such as exposed addresses or initialized padding. Native execution cannot be forced to follow the evaluator’s oracle, so the Core check finds and validates an admissible choice witness per native run, without normalizing away raw observations. Qualifying programs that legitimately observe such values is decided at slice 24. Failed witness search is inconclusive unless exclusion is proved. Compare complete observations: ordered requests/responses/output, terminal classification, trap kind/site/line and observable final external state. Use bounded scripted environments for effectful/blocking cases. Report unsupported or inconclusive runs explicitly; do not count `outOfFuel` as agreement or divergence. Compare leakage separately where the claimed backend evidence supports it. Never require arbitrary user programs to execute three times. Tests qualify implementations; the LCB path is **proved under named premises**, and LLVM remains unverified. Keep the two premise sets separate.

## 4. Revision register

No revision is enacted here. Each question in section 5 has its own answer; an umbrella approval cannot choose these independently.

| Rows | Scope and status | Question |
|---|---|---|
| OD126; Q3.7/Q3.9/Q3.10 | Generalize address/profile interface, preserving provenance rules and zero-byte exception; existing 64-bit little-endian profile unchanged, including Q3.10’s `base + size ≤ 2^64−1` one-past bound. Any generalized profile needs an analogous checked bound. | 3 |
| Q3.5; Q1.11/Q2.4/Q4.6; TYPE-19; FFI-7 | Q3.5 fixes u64 lengths/indices across profiles. Q1.11 rejects counts above u64 as TooLarge; Q2.4 fixes Drained.at: u64; Q4.6 byte offsets/lengths use that index contract. Target-sized usize would revise these and layout-query results. Recommendation retains these semantics and permits only proved narrower representations. | 7 |
| OD126; FFI-7 | Native enum tag/payload layout changes existing i32-tag profile separately from target abstraction. | 4 |
| Q1.7 | Proved release-context erasure, including owning Text where applicable; no unconditional one-pointer claim. | 6 |
| Q7.1/Q7.10; Q9.7 | Lawful registry instances rather than a permanently closed inventory; preserve existing grant contracts. Entropy details deferred. | 16, 14 |
| FFI-7/10 | Natural native struct layout already supersedes unspecified-layout prose under runtime decision; retain repr restrictions. | Retained clarification |
| Q8.1; early-evidence decision; R-0505 | Keep current conditional endpoint and later closure; advancing it requires separate approval. | 8 |

Q9.11/OD144 already decide post-cleanup ensures timing. The SPARK decision already decides source struct invariants. Neither is a new rule. C4’s hybrid/representation completion is a proposed formalization choice, not authority to revise raw access. Exact trap constructors and byte columns are not inferred from Q6.7 or ArithmeticError. OD106/ARITH-10 already fix conversion errors; Q2.3 and Q3.8 already fix destruction orders. Questions 12 and 13 are removed as fully decided; later question numbers are retained for review tracking.

## 5. Separately answerable owner questions

Each recommendation is a proposal, not an implemented language decision. “Defer” preserves existing decided rules and leaves detailed instances to the named slice.

**Q1. Foundation scope.** Recommend approving the interface/law boundary of C1–C10, with freeze only after implementation, kernel proofs, interaction tests, independent review and owner acceptance. Runner-up: freeze smaller integrated scopes incrementally. Neither approves the other numbered questions.
**Q2. Hybrid state and representation completion.** Recommend C4’s structured-place/byte-block state, coherent projection, pointer fragments and explicit initialization/padding laws. Runner-up: use byte blocks for all Core storage. The hybrid keeps typed proofs small while raw operations remain executable; both must preserve Q3.7 and raw observations.
**Q3. Target abstraction.** Recommend read-only profiles separating address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; generalize Q3.7/Q3.9/Q3.10 without changing existing profile constants or reconstruction policy. Runner-up: separate semantics per profile under OD126. No usize or enum change is included.
**Q4. Enum layout.** Recommend retaining the existing profile’s i32 tags for now, deferring a compact u8/u16/u32 algorithm to the aggregate/layout slice and a separate owner answer. Runner-up: approve now the smallest tag fitting the variant count (u8 ≤256, u16 ≤65,536, u32 ≤2^32), declaration-order tags, aligned common payload and rounded aggregate size, rejecting larger counts. This changes OD126/FFI-7 independently of question 3.
**Q5. Niches.** Three options: (a) exactly one null-empty/non-null single-pointer-payload niche, with pointer-size, provenance, ownership and release-context proofs; (b) tagged layout now, future niches only by owner decision; (c) no niches permanently. Recommend **(b)**: the foundation needs representation laws, not a permanent optimization inventory. Runner-up **(a)** gives compact optional ownership but adds a special eligibility proof; multiword release contexts and nullable raw pointers are ineligible. Round-2 reviewers split: Codex and Kimi prefer (b); agy prefers (c). (c) is uniform but unnecessarily permanent.
**Q6. Release-context erasure.** Recommend retaining sufficient stable context and erasing only with a release/identity/dependency preservation proof and no per-instance state. Runner-up: always store context. This separately revises Q1.7; it does not fix Text’s internal word count.
**Q7. usize.** Three options: (a) target-sized usize avoids 64-bit lengths/storage/arithmetic on 8/16/32-bit chips, but changes the cross-profile meaning deliberately fixed by Q3.5 and the dependent contracts in section 4; (b) retain u64 semantics everywhere, with explicit checked target limits, at a cost on small targets; (c) retain those u64 semantics and prove a narrower internal representation wherever bounds justify it, using widening or fallback when they do not. Recommend **(c)**, following [PHILOSOPHY](../docs/PHILOSOPHY.md)’s explicit, checkable semantics and Q3.5. This cannot promise all lengths fit a machine word: zero-sized collections can have large logical counts. Representation narrowing needs a simulation preserving arithmetic, failures and observations, not silent truncation or new target-dependent overflow. Codex and agy favor (a); Kimi favors (b). A new source usize remains a separately approved revision; no isize is implied.
**Q8. Native-image milestone.** Recommend keeping R-0505’s later closure and Q8.1’s conditional endpoint. Runner-up: require proved exact AArch64 linked-image validation by 0.1, explicitly revising Q8.1, early-evidence and proof-infrastructure milestones while retaining all undischarged premises. Qualification is question 17, not bundled here.
**Q9. Trap inventory and spellings.** Recommend deferring concrete constructors/renderings until trap semantics needs them, before their first supported slice. Runner-up: approve `arithmetic(ArithmeticError) | bounds | invalidRange | abort`, rendered `overflow`, `division_by_zero`, `invalid_shift`, `bounds`, `invalid_range`, `abort`, with one trailing newline. ArithmeticError’s decided cases do not decide this wrapper or its strings. Preserve the runtime line format and exits either way. Codex and Kimi support deferral before first use; agy wants the inventory and spellings approved now to avoid churn.
**Q10. Columns and tabs.** Recommend byte columns, tabs counting one byte, with conformance tests; runner-up: Unicode-scalar columns, tabs counting one scalar. The runtime decision fixes only 1-based columns, so neither convention is already decided.
**Q11. UTF-8 failure classification.** Recommend deferring mapping to the text slice’s operation contracts while preserving UTF-8 validity and no hidden traps. Runner-up: map unchecked UTF-8 boundary failures to proposed invalidRange. This does not turn handled checked errors into traps.
**Q14. Entropy shape.** Recommend deferring exact launch-grant type, operations and ending contract to slice 6, consistent with Q7.10 and the randomness schedule. Runner-up: approve a nullary Entropy grant only together with a complete instance contract. No nullary shape is assumed by C5.
**Q15. Struct-invariant implementation.** Recommend deferring the source feature to slice 17, while writing the decided SPARK exposure definition in the spec before freeze and retaining Q9.11/OD144 post-cleanup ensures semantics. Runner-up: implement the source feature earlier if an interaction proof needs it. There is no new timing choice to approve.
**Q16. Registry extensibility.** Recommend lawful typed extensions preserving existing primitive/grant entries and exhaustive coverage of every selected instance; unknown operations are refused. Runner-up: retain closed Q9.7/Q7.10 inventories and seek an owner amendment for each addition. Schema compatibility must preserve old records; no new source spelling is implied.
**Q17. Differential qualification.** Recommend keeping the decided [backend-order](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06) rule unchanged: on every supported example, LLVM and LCB full observations must equal each other and the expected outcome, and any mismatch fails. Add, on top of it, the check that each native trace is an allowed observation of the Core semantics. Supported examples must not observe allocation-dependent values (exposed addresses, initialized padding), so equality stays meaningful. How to qualify programs that legitimately observe such values (raw pointers, slice 24) is an open question for that slice, not relaxed now. Runner-up: relax cross-backend equality to per-backend refinement for such programs now, which revises the backend-order row. Kimi found draft 3 relaxed that decided rule without registering it; this fixes that.

**Q18. Secret<T> layout.** Recommend that `Secret<T>` has exactly the layout of T (zero-cost wrapper). Layout does not establish constant time; the information-flow rules do. The [information-flow decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06) covers typing, Copy, wiping and leakage, not representation, so this is a new rule (Kimi, round 3). Runner-up: an opaque representation fixed per slice. All three reviewers chose identical layout in the earlier step-3 round.

